#uuid "f6b3c3e6-ef30-562b-8daf-eeaea2a83e72" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186704608}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "59f5cc26-3be0-5dc2-bf7a-9e1cbb33a3f3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186704580} :answer-field/id #uuid "419bcb17-6e62-5440-80d6-13aacc3d5238" :answer-field/key "$\\operatorname{curl}\\mathbf F=\\mathbf 0$ everywhere" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186704574} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "28d7c28e-80f5-5ca5-91ed-742d85ed442b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186704569} {:answer/id #uuid "81fb7e48-c7c7-511d-964a-2a57f72966ce" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186704589}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "28d7c28e-80f5-5ca5-91ed-742d85ed442b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186704569} :answer-field/id #uuid "74bb0c2c-14e8-5d02-898f-0997541d026e" :answer-field/key "$\\operatorname{curl}\\mathbf F\\ne\\mathbf 0$ everywhere" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186704587}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "48be0430-2eca-5fab-a46a-cb09e24f1865" :question/problem "![Vector field; arrows share one scale.](images/68/688d9a7587ff23cad27de658b4d4e05b7600cf25126ef2b17b30f32fba11df24.png)\n\nThe diagram shows a vector field $\\mathbf F$ on $\\mathbb R^3$. Its $z$-component is zero and it is independent of $z$. View the $xy$-plane from the positive $z$-axis, so positive $z$ points out of the page. Both planar components are affine functions, and the displayed pattern continues throughout the domain.\n\nAll vectors are vertical. The vertical component increases at a constant rate as $x$ increases, and does not change with the other coordinate.\n\nWhich statements are true everywhere? Select all that apply." :question/worked-solution "Imagine a small paddlewheel with its axle parallel to the $z$-axis.\n\nThe horizontal flow is unchanged between the upper and lower sides of a wheel, so it contributes no rotation.\n\nThe vertical flow is more upward to the right of a wheel than to its left. This contributes a **counterclockwise** turn.\n\nThe wheel turns **counterclockwise** everywhere, so $\\operatorname{curl}\\mathbf F\\ne\\mathbf 0$ everywhere. Wrap the fingers of your right hand counterclockwise: the thumb points out of the page, in the **positive $z$-direction**. The field is **not irrotational**.\n\n- $\\operatorname{curl}\\mathbf F=\\mathbf 0$ everywhere: **false**.\n\n- $\\mathbf F$ is irrotational: **false**.\n\n- $\\operatorname{curl}\\mathbf F\\ne\\mathbf 0$ everywhere: **true**.\n\n- $\\operatorname{curl}\\mathbf F$ points in the positive $z$-direction everywhere: **true**.\n\nThe comparison below contrasts unequal sideways pushes with equal pushes.\n\n![](images/59/594106633e422d24b9fea113d24fb6367fdef5ac52724931860e4163e33ce992.png)"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186704585 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "69d885f7-b9c4-5d58-bc6b-43dc432014ef" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186704583} {:answer/id #uuid "acb3db17-94db-5ef9-b404-0d09a4541f16" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186704597}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "69d885f7-b9c4-5d58-bc6b-43dc432014ef" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186704583} :answer-field/id #uuid "1973c7ec-a70d-5813-b233-b6e7adf4f879" :answer-field/key "$\\operatorname{curl}\\mathbf F\\ne\\mathbf 0$ everywhere" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186704565} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "3fb10568-0a65-5092-9a26-b71468f08b15" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186704573} {:answer/id #uuid "95f14a32-f2a2-5bf4-8b10-78a598b9ed50" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186704594}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "95f14a32-f2a2-5bf4-8b10-78a598b9ed50" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186704594} :answer-field/id #uuid "3924daf7-37e5-55f1-83d1-9e402f236700" :answer-field/key "$\\operatorname{curl}\\mathbf F$ points in the negative $z$-direction everywhere" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186704572} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "98ff198a-22ca-5738-bd7c-5ef2e780aa14" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186704595} {:answer/id #uuid "d4e3943b-64ec-5457-b23e-3fe1a2682663" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186704603}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "98ff198a-22ca-5738-bd7c-5ef2e780aa14" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186704595} :answer-field/id #uuid "d1d25a92-3dab-57a1-9629-910fe9f428eb" :answer-field/key "$\\operatorname{curl}\\mathbf F=\\mathbf 0$ everywhere" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186704602} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "025800e2-227b-5622-99a4-0c74f6203099" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186704558} {:answer/id #uuid "cf81ae7d-a942-5c48-9e20-56c2fe13fdf2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186704601}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "cf81ae7d-a942-5c48-9e20-56c2fe13fdf2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186704601} :answer-field/id #uuid "dbafa553-9a97-50fe-b675-a74c3fe9011b" :answer-field/key "$\\mathbf F$ is irrotational" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186704605}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "731ab35b-954f-50a4-8f4a-0f2825a17e1f" :question/problem "![Vector field; arrows share one scale.](images/01/013ee8bc237f52d5487745d32840917046ad59f7c6b1848327e65ad463c7a4ff.png)\n\nThe diagram shows a vector field $\\mathbf F$ on $\\mathbb R^3$. Its $z$-component is zero and it is independent of $z$. View the $xy$-plane from the positive $z$-axis, so positive $z$ points out of the page. Both planar components are affine functions, and the displayed pattern continues throughout the domain.\n\nAll vectors are horizontal. The horizontal component decreases at a constant rate as $y$ increases, and does not change with the other coordinate.\n\nWhich statements are true everywhere? Select all that apply." :question/worked-solution "Imagine a small paddlewheel with its axle parallel to the $z$-axis.\n\nThe horizontal flow is more leftward above a wheel than below it. This contributes a **counterclockwise** turn.\n\nThe vertical flow is unchanged between the right and left sides of a wheel, so it contributes no rotation.\n\nA constant flow can carry the wheel along without changing its spin.\n\nThe wheel turns **counterclockwise** everywhere, so $\\operatorname{curl}\\mathbf F\\ne\\mathbf 0$ everywhere. Wrap the fingers of your right hand counterclockwise: the thumb points out of the page, in the **positive $z$-direction**. The field is **not irrotational**.\n\n- $\\operatorname{curl}\\mathbf F\\ne\\mathbf 0$ everywhere: **true**.\n\n- $\\operatorname{curl}\\mathbf F=\\mathbf 0$ everywhere: **false**.\n\n- $\\mathbf F$ is irrotational: **false**.\n\n- $\\operatorname{curl}\\mathbf F$ points in the negative $z$-direction everywhere: **false**.\n\nThe comparison below contrasts unequal sideways pushes with equal pushes.\n\n![](images/59/594106633e422d24b9fea113d24fb6367fdef5ac52724931860e4163e33ce992.png)"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186704829 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "e749ef30-77c5-59c3-a1e0-393b936f7f2d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{x^{9}}{9}+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186704838}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "e749ef30-77c5-59c3-a1e0-393b936f7f2d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{x^{9}}{9}+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186704838} :answer-field/id #uuid "5af7e46e-708f-50c8-89ad-c257876e468a" :answer-field/key "antiderivative" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186704831}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "3b6a9d78-be17-5fe2-a3f6-8ea8a23ef0fb" :question/problem "Calculate \n\n$$\n\\int {x}^{8}\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\n Use $C$ for the arbitrary constant of integration.\n\n{{antiderivative}}" :question/worked-solution "Apply the power rule with $n=8$ and include an arbitrary constant $C$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int x^{8}\\,\\mathrm{d}x&=\\frac{x^{8+1}}{8+1}+C \\\\ &=\\frac{x^{9}}{9}+C.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nCheck by differentiating:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\mathrm{d}}{\\mathrm{d}x}\\left(\\frac{x^{9}}{9}+C\\right)&=\\frac{1}{9}\\cdot 9x^{8}+0 \\\\ &=x^{8}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186704833 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1c62d47e-98ee-5481-8f1d-4be4b02aada5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{x^{16}}{16}+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186704828}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "1c62d47e-98ee-5481-8f1d-4be4b02aada5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{x^{16}}{16}+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186704828} :answer-field/id #uuid "c89b9c86-d516-580d-a990-693b697834bc" :answer-field/key "antiderivative" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186704834}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "90f14c3f-45af-5278-9957-6b92ec8354d6" :question/problem "Calculate \n\n$$\n\\int {x}^{15}\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\n Use $C$ for the arbitrary constant of integration.\n\n{{antiderivative}}" :question/worked-solution "Apply the power rule with $n=15$ and include an arbitrary constant $C$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int x^{15}\\,\\mathrm{d}x&=\\frac{x^{15+1}}{15+1}+C \\\\ &=\\frac{x^{16}}{16}+C.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nCheck by differentiating:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\mathrm{d}}{\\mathrm{d}x}\\left(\\frac{x^{16}}{16}+C\\right)&=\\frac{1}{16}\\cdot 16x^{15}+0 \\\\ &=x^{15}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186704835 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "8a1627df-e486-501b-a8f0-4a9e394dc74e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{x^{20}}{20}+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186704832}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "8a1627df-e486-501b-a8f0-4a9e394dc74e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{x^{20}}{20}+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186704832} :answer-field/id #uuid "456088ff-55fa-5e43-b60a-c99ef8a22e5a" :answer-field/key "antiderivative" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186704830}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "d00eb424-29f8-51c1-83a9-f9d0e59fbee0" :question/problem "Calculate $F(x)$ given that $f(x)={x}^{19}$ and ${F}^{'}(x)=f(x).$ Use $C$ for the arbitrary constant of integration.\n\n$F(x)=$ {{antiderivative}}" :question/worked-solution "Integration reverses differentiation, so $F'(x)=f(x)$ gives $F(x)=\\int f(x)\\,\\mathrm{d}x$.\n\nApply the power rule with $n=19$ and include an arbitrary constant $C$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F(x)&=\\int x^{19}\\,\\mathrm{d}x \\\\ &=\\frac{x^{19+1}}{19+1}+C \\\\ &=\\frac{x^{20}}{20}+C.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nCheck by differentiating:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\mathrm{d}}{\\mathrm{d}x}\\left(\\frac{x^{20}}{20}+C\\right)&=\\frac{1}{20}\\cdot 20x^{19}+0 \\\\ &=x^{19}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186704837 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "d8810d54-d9e5-5b7c-883c-1fd4e263c50c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{x^{2}}{2}+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186704836}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "d8810d54-d9e5-5b7c-883c-1fd4e263c50c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{x^{2}}{2}+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186704836} :answer-field/id #uuid "e766b1b7-9dae-567a-9120-fb22041ad40f" :answer-field/key "antiderivative" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186704839}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "df16da36-a902-5c17-9016-49db16dba219" :question/problem "Calculate \n\n$$\n\\int {x}^{1}\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\n Use $C$ for the arbitrary constant of integration.\n\n{{antiderivative}}" :question/worked-solution "Apply the power rule with $n=1$ and include an arbitrary constant $C$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int x\\,\\mathrm{d}x&=\\frac{x^{1+1}}{1+1}+C \\\\ &=\\frac{x^{2}}{2}+C.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nCheck by differentiating:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\mathrm{d}}{\\mathrm{d}x}\\left(\\frac{x^{2}}{2}+C\\right)&=\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot 2x+0 \\\\ &=x.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186706065 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "91a7d8c4-5619-5015-9a46-5764e5bea2b8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{2}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186706074} {:answer/id #uuid "9e811ec6-7a0a-5799-98f4-51a07af396b0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186706077} {:answer/id #uuid "c229d3a8-b78f-5b89-9121-85926b037fd7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186706078} {:answer/id #uuid "e0628799-ae4c-58c6-b9da-cd2dced6c518" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{4}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186706081} {:answer/id #uuid "eb7a3988-fd2b-5fd5-9a89-9f3407cd3ad7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{2}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186706082}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "eb7a3988-fd2b-5fd5-9a89-9f3407cd3ad7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{2}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186706082} :answer-field/id #uuid "9da1d2ea-94ca-596c-b1ea-f99bba013658" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186706076}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "145a65a8-1a08-5dbb-8b14-af3521153cef" :question/problem "Evaluate\n$$\n\\displaystyle \\int_0^{\\pi/2}\\int_0^{\\sin\\theta}2r\\sin\\theta\\,\\mathrm d r\\,\\mathrm d\\theta.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "First, integrate with respect to $r$, treating $\\theta$ as a constant. Substitute the angular expression for the upper radial limit only after finding the antiderivative:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nI&=\\int_0^{\\pi/2}\\sin\\theta\\left[r^2\\right]_0^{\\sin\\theta}\\,\\mathrm d\\theta\\\\\n&=\\int_0^{\\pi/2}\\sin^3\\theta\\,\\mathrm d\\theta.\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo integrate the odd power, use $\\sin^2\\theta=1-\\cos^2\\theta$. With $u=\\cos\\theta$ and $\\mathrm d u=-\\sin\\theta\\,\\mathrm d\\theta$, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nI&=\\int_0^{\\pi/2}\\sin\\theta(1-\\cos^2\\theta)\\,\\mathrm d\\theta\\\\\n&=\\left[-\\cos\\theta+\\frac{\\cos^3\\theta}{3}\\right]_0^{\\pi/2}\\\\\n&=0-\\left(-1+\\frac13\\right)=\\frac23.\n\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186706068 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "30c90017-66c5-533e-9572-89f5664270a1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{17}{8}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186706066} {:answer/id #uuid "512c6f6f-9331-5d68-b7d5-d0d8ed93d2cf" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{15}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186706069} {:answer/id #uuid "5f1aecb9-518a-59ad-964e-93470f43b6bd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{15}{8}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186706070} {:answer/id #uuid "cf284fcf-a98a-5c19-9e90-d43cd32bcb41" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{15}{16}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186706079} {:answer/id #uuid "f32a00c1-3c45-51cf-9046-fdc6c0a10991" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186706083}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "5f1aecb9-518a-59ad-964e-93470f43b6bd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{15}{8}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186706070} :answer-field/id #uuid "6e037475-2345-537a-8fe5-c66c6f601cdc" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186706072}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "4a91f6cb-2b76-5b7b-b38b-4ce15864967a" :question/problem "Evaluate\n$$\n\\displaystyle \\int_0^{\\pi/2}\\int_1^2 r^3\\sin\\theta\\cos\\theta\\,\\mathrm d r\\,\\mathrm d\\theta.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "First, integrate with respect to $r$, treating $\\theta$ as a constant:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nI&=\\int_0^{\\pi/2}\\sin\\theta\\cos\\theta\n\\left[\\frac{r^4}{4}\\right]_1^2\\,\\mathrm d\\theta\\\\\n&=\\frac{15}{4}\\int_0^{\\pi/2}\\sin\\theta\\cos\\theta\\,\\mathrm d\\theta.\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, integrate with respect to $\\theta$. Since the derivative of $\\sin^2\\theta$ is $2\\sin\\theta\\cos\\theta$,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nI&=\\frac{15}{8}\\left[\\sin^2\\theta\\right]_0^{\\pi/2}\\\\\n&=\\frac{15}{8}(1-0)=\\frac{15}{8}.\n\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186706075 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "02f76c1c-75b9-53a9-9cb7-360357c89ac6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{7\\sqrt3}{16}-\\frac{\\pi}{12}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186706063} {:answer/id #uuid "32439562-c012-5028-89d3-6e70ae793c86" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{7\\sqrt3}{32}-\\frac{\\pi}{24}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186706067} {:answer/id #uuid "61eb6d02-df78-5c09-9327-d287accdb7a2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{7\\sqrt3}{32}+\\frac{\\pi}{24}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186706071} {:answer/id #uuid "7e318cf3-6a58-5b39-9933-7d9be02a6596" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\pi}{24}+\\frac{\\sqrt3}{32}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186706073} {:answer/id #uuid "d3811688-a37d-5813-b97f-e458bad13426" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\sqrt3}{4}-\\frac{\\pi}{24}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186706080}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "32439562-c012-5028-89d3-6e70ae793c86" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{7\\sqrt3}{32}-\\frac{\\pi}{24}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186706067} :answer-field/id #uuid "070c47e4-3c95-5541-9629-c9dfad19285e" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186706064}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "9ac8d21e-d718-5ba6-bec2-fc44e97a38f9" :question/problem "Evaluate\n$$\n\\displaystyle \\int_0^{\\pi/6}\\int_{1-\\cos(2\\theta)}^1 r\\,\\mathrm d r\\,\\mathrm d\\theta.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "First, integrate with respect to $r$, treating $\\theta$ as a constant. The lower radial limit must be squared as a whole:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nI&=\\frac12\\int_0^{\\pi/6}\\left(1-(1-\\cos(2\\theta))^2\\right)\\,\\mathrm d\\theta\\\\\n&=\\int_0^{\\pi/6}\\left(\\cos(2\\theta)-\\frac12\\cos^2(2\\theta)\\right)\\,\\mathrm d\\theta.\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, recall $\\cos(2x)=2\\cos^2 x-1$. Substituting $x=2\\theta$ and rearranging gives\n\n$$\n\\cos^2(2\\theta)=\\frac{1+\\cos(4\\theta)}{2}.\n$$\n\nUse this identity and integrate with respect to $\\theta$, including the factors from the angular frequencies:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nI&=\\int_0^{\\pi/6}\\left(\\cos(2\\theta)-\\frac14-\\frac14\\cos(4\\theta)\\right)\\,\\mathrm d\\theta\\\\\n&=\\left[\\frac{\\sin(2\\theta)}{2}-\\frac{\\theta}{4}-\\frac{\\sin(4\\theta)}{16}\\right]_0^{\\pi/6}.\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nBoth sine terms survive at the upper endpoint: $\\sin(\\pi/3)=\\sin(2\\pi/3)=\\sqrt3/2$. All three terms vanish at the lower endpoint. Therefore,\n\n$$\nI=\\frac{\\sqrt3}{4}-\\frac{\\pi}{24}-\\frac{\\sqrt3}{32}\n=\\frac{7\\sqrt3}{32}-\\frac{\\pi}{24}.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186706862 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "188fb8d0-26c0-5911-9c05-6fe024c2aa2b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\displaystyle\\int_{0}^{\\pi}\\int_{0}^{2\\sin\\theta}r\\,\\mathrm{d}r\\,\\mathrm{d}\\theta" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186706858} {:answer/id #uuid "62b7ce87-157e-58ca-9bc8-538b8d2b8b38" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\displaystyle\\int_{0}^{2\\pi}\\int_{0}^{2\\cos\\theta}r\\,\\mathrm{d}r\\,\\mathrm{d}\\theta" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186706864} {:answer/id #uuid "6c131705-35b9-5376-b9a9-bc160261b088" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\displaystyle\\int_{-\\pi/2}^{\\pi/2}\\int_{0}^{1}r\\,\\mathrm{d}r\\,\\mathrm{d}\\theta" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186706865} {:answer/id #uuid "89a266e9-c6b6-5ae1-9c4b-5a5ea212920e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\displaystyle\\int_{-\\pi/2}^{\\pi/2}\\int_{0}^{2\\cos\\theta}r\\,\\mathrm{d}r\\,\\mathrm{d}\\theta" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186706867} {:answer/id #uuid "e8e34cfb-f464-515b-a6aa-28e3ff4f6187" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\displaystyle\\int_{-\\pi/2}^{\\pi/2}\\int_{0}^{2\\cos\\theta}1\\,\\mathrm{d}r\\,\\mathrm{d}\\theta" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186706875}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "89a266e9-c6b6-5ae1-9c4b-5a5ea212920e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\displaystyle\\int_{-\\pi/2}^{\\pi/2}\\int_{0}^{2\\cos\\theta}r\\,\\mathrm{d}r\\,\\mathrm{d}\\theta" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186706867} :answer-field/id #uuid "b9493c77-f201-5544-9609-03b979ec41f9" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186706872}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "4d408f39-2e46-52fb-9f53-9b9f8b24dfbc" :question/problem "Let $D=\\{(x,y):(x-1)^2+y^2\\leq1\\}$. The disk is shown below.\n\n![Disk of radius 1 centered at (1,0)](images/14/1417d15b6192e34275ab2688f5861233f8a6c337b84d3a7952a31951f88e35ef.png)\n\nIts outer circular boundary has polar equation $r=2\\cos\\theta$. Use $x=r\\cos\\theta$, $y=r\\sin\\theta$, and $\\mathrm{d}A=r\\,\\mathrm{d}r\\,\\mathrm{d}\\theta$ to rewrite\n\n$$\n\\iint_D 1\\,\\mathrm{d}A.\n$$\n\nSelect the polar-coordinate setup whose bounds cover $D$ once with $r\\geq0$ and whose integrand includes the area factor. Do not evaluate the integral." :question/worked-solution "The disk lies to the right of the origin. Rays through its interior have $-\\pi/2<\\theta<\\pi/2$; the endpoint rays contribute only the origin. Along each ray, $r$ starts at $0$ and ends at the supplied boundary $2\\cos\\theta$.\n\n![Radial segment from the origin to the circle and angular sweep through the right half-plane](images/04/0406772f24681d58c80d68bb8bd82e9cb5b9931c5faad8078c683d5e60283964.png)\n\nThe density is $1$, so including the area factor gives $1\\cdot r=r$. Hence\n\n$$\n\\iint_D 1\\,\\mathrm{d}A=\n\\displaystyle\\int_{-\\pi/2}^{\\pi/2}\\int_{0}^{2\\cos\\theta}r\\,\\mathrm{d}r\\,\\mathrm{d}\\theta.\n$$\n\nThe number $1$ is the radius measured from the disk's center; it is not the polar distance from the origin to every boundary point. Omitting $r$ loses the area factor. A full angular turn includes negative upper radial bounds, and $2\\sin\\theta$ describes a different disk."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186706868 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "031e5f0f-9686-5f09-9e5a-5a63373bf65a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\displaystyle\\int_{\\pi}^{2\\pi}\\int_{0}^{1}r^3\\sin^2\\theta\\,\\mathrm{d}r\\,\\mathrm{d}\\theta" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186706855} {:answer/id #uuid "0a23d0f1-cff2-50e2-bba4-315ee9c4a777" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\displaystyle\\int_{\\pi}^{2\\pi}\\int_{0}^{2\\sin\\theta}r^3\\sin^2\\theta\\,\\mathrm{d}r\\,\\mathrm{d}\\theta" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186706856} {:answer/id #uuid "177f6f42-c671-50df-a9d1-9dc69a9c4842" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\displaystyle\\int_{\\pi}^{2\\pi}\\int_{0}^{-2\\sin\\theta}r^3\\sin^2\\theta\\,\\mathrm{d}r\\,\\mathrm{d}\\theta" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186706857} {:answer/id #uuid "1a829f78-d5a0-587f-8d79-d1f548a0be4a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\displaystyle\\int_{0}^{2\\pi}\\int_{0}^{-2\\sin\\theta}r^3\\sin^2\\theta\\,\\mathrm{d}r\\,\\mathrm{d}\\theta" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186706859} {:answer/id #uuid "cfc388d3-ff93-5b5d-8c48-e6d4846d4146" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\displaystyle\\int_{\\pi}^{2\\pi}\\int_{0}^{-2\\sin\\theta}r^2\\sin^2\\theta\\,\\mathrm{d}r\\,\\mathrm{d}\\theta" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186706874}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "177f6f42-c671-50df-a9d1-9dc69a9c4842" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\displaystyle\\int_{\\pi}^{2\\pi}\\int_{0}^{-2\\sin\\theta}r^3\\sin^2\\theta\\,\\mathrm{d}r\\,\\mathrm{d}\\theta" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186706857} :answer-field/id #uuid "2eea323f-23e0-53ec-85fc-38e525aba4bf" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186706860}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "8fd95ea8-5612-5d31-8c5d-ece96690912b" :question/problem "Let $D=\\{(x,y):x^2+(y+1)^2\\leq1\\}$. Rewrite\n\n$$\n\\iint_D y^2\\,\\mathrm{d}A.\n$$\n\nSelect the polar-coordinate setup whose bounds cover $D$ once with $r\\geq0$ and whose integrand includes the area factor. Do not evaluate the integral." :question/worked-solution "Use $x=r\\cos\\theta$, $y=r\\sin\\theta$, and $\\mathrm{d}A=r\\,\\mathrm{d}r\\,\\mathrm{d}\\theta$.\n\nThe region is a disk of radius $1$ centered at $(0,-1)$, tangent to the origin.\n\n![Disk of radius 1 centered at (0,-1)](images/55/557e8cd5bcabc2e0aaa9410be010a37d56682c1c5bd1a9909f27fdb4cdde8c35.png)\n\nExpanding the disk inequality and substituting polar coordinates gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nx^2+y^2+2y&\\leq0\\\\\nr^2+2r\\sin\\theta&\\leq0\\\\\nr(r+2\\sin\\theta)&\\leq0.\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFor $r>0$, this means $r\\leq-2\\sin\\theta$; the origin is also included. The rays entering the disk lie below the $x$-axis, so choose $\\pi\\leq\\theta\\leq2\\pi$. On this interval $-2\\sin\\theta\\geq0$.\n\n![Radial segment and angular sweep through the lower half-plane](images/bf/bf7eb129a2dfa2163083399637b02f06e486966bbd2a88c6824172d873b6297a.png)\n\nThe transformed density and area element are\n\n$$\ny^2\\,\\mathrm{d}A=(r\\sin\\theta)^2r\\,\\mathrm{d}r\\,\\mathrm{d}\\theta\n=r^3\\sin^2\\theta\\,\\mathrm{d}r\\,\\mathrm{d}\\theta.\n$$\n\nTherefore\n\n$$\n\\iint_D y^2\\,\\mathrm{d}A=\n\\displaystyle\\int_{\\pi}^{2\\pi}\\int_{0}^{-2\\sin\\theta}r^3\\sin^2\\theta\\,\\mathrm{d}r\\,\\mathrm{d}\\theta.\n$$\n\nUsing $r^2\\sin^2\\theta$ omits the area factor. Reversing the radial sign makes the upper bound negative here. A full turn includes rays outside $D$, and the constant upper bound $1$ would use distance from the wrong center."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186706873 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "4a3732d5-837d-520b-a967-615e9d2ada6e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\displaystyle\\int_{\\pi/2}^{\\pi}\\int_{0}^{2\\cos\\theta}r e^{r^2\\cos(2\\theta)}\\,\\mathrm{d}r\\,\\mathrm{d}\\theta" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186706861} {:answer/id #uuid "7238ba16-0130-5a4f-a97c-082570b01fd4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\displaystyle\\int_{\\pi/2}^{\\pi}\\int_{0}^{-2\\cos\\theta}r e^{r^2\\cos(2\\theta)}\\,\\mathrm{d}r\\,\\mathrm{d}\\theta" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186706866} {:answer/id #uuid "98ff85e3-68c9-5de9-b093-f29c240ab27e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\displaystyle\\int_{\\pi/2}^{\\pi}\\int_{0}^{-2\\cos\\theta}e^{r^2\\cos(2\\theta)}\\,\\mathrm{d}r\\,\\mathrm{d}\\theta" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186706869} {:answer/id #uuid "9b42f45e-e570-58a1-abdd-a87b1cccc635" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\displaystyle\\int_{\\pi/2}^{3\\pi/2}\\int_{0}^{-2\\cos\\theta}r e^{r^2\\cos(2\\theta)}\\,\\mathrm{d}r\\,\\mathrm{d}\\theta" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186706870} {:answer/id #uuid "a883b850-8572-54df-81b6-e1869f3083f9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\displaystyle\\int_{\\pi/2}^{\\pi}\\int_{0}^{-2\\cos\\theta}r e^{r^2}\\,\\mathrm{d}r\\,\\mathrm{d}\\theta" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186706871}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "7238ba16-0130-5a4f-a97c-082570b01fd4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\displaystyle\\int_{\\pi/2}^{\\pi}\\int_{0}^{-2\\cos\\theta}r e^{r^2\\cos(2\\theta)}\\,\\mathrm{d}r\\,\\mathrm{d}\\theta" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186706866} :answer-field/id #uuid "593ee4d4-2968-563c-889a-44a9b044dd7a" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186706863}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "bcbbfb31-c08e-5791-b436-0158e20d95fe" :question/problem "Let $D=\\{(x,y):(x+1)^2+y^2\\leq1,\\ y\\geq0\\}$. Rewrite\n\n$$\n\\iint_D e^{x^2-y^2}\\,\\mathrm{d}A.\n$$\n\nSelect the polar-coordinate setup whose bounds cover $D$ once with $r\\geq0$ and whose integrand includes the area factor. Do not evaluate the integral." :question/worked-solution "Use $x=r\\cos\\theta$, $y=r\\sin\\theta$, and $\\mathrm{d}A=r\\,\\mathrm{d}r\\,\\mathrm{d}\\theta$.\n\nThe full disk has radius $1$ and center $(-1,0)$. The restriction $y\\geq0$ keeps its upper half.\n\n![Upper half of the disk centered at (-1,0)](images/db/dbe2693400c48eed005fc583951553c1245fbecd133cfcf95c443802ed577b86.png)\n\nThe circular inequality becomes\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nx^2+y^2+2x&\\leq0\\\\\nr^2+2r\\cos\\theta&\\leq0\\\\\nr(r+2\\cos\\theta)&\\leq0.\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThus $0\\leq r\\leq-2\\cos\\theta$. The full disk requires $\\cos\\theta\\leq0$, which permits $\\pi/2\\leq\\theta\\leq3\\pi/2$. The additional condition $y=r\\sin\\theta\\geq0$ restricts us to $\\pi/2\\leq\\theta\\leq\\pi$.\n\n![Radial segment and angular sweep restricted to the second quadrant](images/37/37e7ef8fa8e628bb866e176fcfa0b19105d5d738d80b339b45b633246793cf18.png)\n\nTransform the difference in the exponent before including the area factor:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nx^2-y^2&=r^2(\\cos^2\\theta-\\sin^2\\theta)\\\\\n&=r^2\\cos(2\\theta),\\\\\ne^{x^2-y^2}\\,\\mathrm{d}A&=r e^{r^2\\cos(2\\theta)}\\,\\mathrm{d}r\\,\\mathrm{d}\\theta.\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore\n\n$$\n\\iint_D e^{x^2-y^2}\\,\\mathrm{d}A=\n\\displaystyle\\int_{\\pi/2}^{\\pi}\\int_{0}^{-2\\cos\\theta}r e^{r^2\\cos(2\\theta)}\\,\\mathrm{d}r\\,\\mathrm{d}\\theta.\n$$\n\nThe full left-half-plane sweep would include the lower half of the disk. On the chosen interval, $2\\cos\\theta$ has the wrong sign. Dropping $r$ omits the area factor, and replacing the exponent by $r^2$ incorrectly treats a difference of squares as a sum."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186706883 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/feedback "Omitted the Jacobian factor $r$." :answer/id #uuid "1e7e27f6-dd22-51d4-98eb-250dfe7f4b4b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\displaystyle\\int_{0}^{2\\pi}\\int_{0}^{2}r^2\\sin^2\\theta\\,\\mathrm{d}r\\,\\mathrm{d}\\theta" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186706884} {:answer/feedback "Used the substitution for $x^2$ instead of $y^2$." :answer/id #uuid "82ee3201-7b02-56fa-96f6-06fad390fa95" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\displaystyle\\int_{0}^{2\\pi}\\int_{0}^{2}r^3\\cos^2\\theta\\,\\mathrm{d}r\\,\\mathrm{d}\\theta" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186706892} {:answer/feedback "Used the squared radius as the radial bound." :answer/id #uuid "83b471e0-cb55-549c-8cc8-5e13d6d8b1b2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\displaystyle\\int_{0}^{2\\pi}\\int_{0}^{4}r^3\\sin^2\\theta\\,\\mathrm{d}r\\,\\mathrm{d}\\theta" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186706893} {:answer/feedback "Covered only half of the disk." :answer/id #uuid "bbd8d568-35a2-5901-a9e8-3fa5c56574a0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\displaystyle\\int_{0}^{\\pi}\\int_{0}^{2}r^3\\sin^2\\theta\\,\\mathrm{d}r\\,\\mathrm{d}\\theta" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186706896} {:answer/feedback "The bounds, substituted integrand, and polar area factor are all correct." :answer/id #uuid "e5a7fdd8-1732-5d11-bf47-f290917badb6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\displaystyle\\int_{0}^{2\\pi}\\int_{0}^{2}r^3\\sin^2\\theta\\,\\mathrm{d}r\\,\\mathrm{d}\\theta" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186706899}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/feedback "The bounds, substituted integrand, and polar area factor are all correct." :answer/id #uuid "e5a7fdd8-1732-5d11-bf47-f290917badb6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\displaystyle\\int_{0}^{2\\pi}\\int_{0}^{2}r^3\\sin^2\\theta\\,\\mathrm{d}r\\,\\mathrm{d}\\theta" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186706899} :answer-field/id #uuid "ebdc895d-62b0-54fe-be4b-2d74b166e036" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186706900}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "1147f2c6-523b-5f9e-b34e-eade19826476" :question/problem "![Shaded disk centered at the origin](images/55/5591e60602a79383a0242dc2f588ee0a4553de5e949c1ae80abaf5821ac0a7e7.png)\n\nThe region $D$ is the shaded disk shown above. Rewrite\n\n$$\n\\iint_D y^2\\,\\mathrm{d}A\n$$\n\nin polar coordinates. Choose the setup whose bounds describe $D$ with $r\\ge0$ and whose integrand includes the polar area factor. Do not evaluate the integral." :question/worked-solution "Use $x=r\\cos\\theta$, $y=r\\sin\\theta$, and $\\mathrm{d}A=r\\,\\mathrm{d}r\\,\\mathrm{d}\\theta$. The extra factor of $r$ comes from the area element.\n\nThe circle has radius $2$ and is centered at the origin. A full turn covers the disk once, so\n\n$$\n0\\le r\\le2,\\qquad 0\\le\\theta\\le2\\pi\n$$\n\nSubstitute for $y$ and include the area factor:\n\n\n$$\ny^2\\,\\mathrm{d}A=(r\\sin\\theta)^2r\\,\\mathrm{d}r\\,\\mathrm{d}\\theta=r^3\\sin^2\\theta\\,\\mathrm{d}r\\,\\mathrm{d}\\theta.\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\iint_D y^2\\,\\mathrm{d}A=\\displaystyle\\int_{0}^{2\\pi}\\int_{0}^{2}r^3\\sin^2\\theta\\,\\mathrm{d}r\\,\\mathrm{d}\\theta.\n$$\n\n"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186706890 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/feedback "Omitted the Jacobian factor $r$." :answer/id #uuid "244074ba-67be-5748-bb6f-d27176b7a612" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\displaystyle\\int_{\\pi/2}^{\\pi}\\int_{0}^{2}r^3\\sin(2\\theta)\\,\\mathrm{d}r\\,\\mathrm{d}\\theta" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186706886} {:answer/feedback "The bounds, substituted integrand, and polar area factor are all correct." :answer/id #uuid "8782fc65-c32d-5906-84ea-58093c623c9c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\displaystyle\\int_{\\pi/2}^{\\pi}\\int_{0}^{2}r^4\\sin(2\\theta)\\,\\mathrm{d}r\\,\\mathrm{d}\\theta" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186706894} {:answer/feedback "Used $r^2$ for $\\sqrt{r^2}$; for $r\\ge0$, the square root is $r$." :answer/id #uuid "9f01857e-ec07-5625-9c23-b07e4babdc3c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\displaystyle\\int_{\\pi/2}^{\\pi}\\int_{0}^{2}r^5\\sin(2\\theta)\\,\\mathrm{d}r\\,\\mathrm{d}\\theta" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186706895} {:answer/feedback "Lost the factor $2$ in the double-angle identity." :answer/id #uuid "bd5641b3-b65e-59fc-a088-ab34323a26f2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\displaystyle\\int_{\\pi/2}^{\\pi}\\int_{0}^{2}r^4\\sin\\theta\\cos\\theta\\,\\mathrm{d}r\\,\\mathrm{d}\\theta" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186706897} {:answer/feedback "These angles describe the first quadrant." :answer/id #uuid "fc755221-bfdc-5bd3-971b-62110436b9a0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\displaystyle\\int_{0}^{\\pi/2}\\int_{0}^{2}r^4\\sin(2\\theta)\\,\\mathrm{d}r\\,\\mathrm{d}\\theta" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186706901}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/feedback "The bounds, substituted integrand, and polar area factor are all correct." :answer/id #uuid "8782fc65-c32d-5906-84ea-58093c623c9c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\displaystyle\\int_{\\pi/2}^{\\pi}\\int_{0}^{2}r^4\\sin(2\\theta)\\,\\mathrm{d}r\\,\\mathrm{d}\\theta" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186706894} :answer-field/id #uuid "ff306da3-1f36-5a1d-8fe4-8b78e4d15acc" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186706903}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "49b88ca0-f3e1-5a24-9566-7f5353efe0ba" :question/problem "![Shaded quarter-disk centered at the origin](images/2a/2ae1a2cecf6a51569db0beec997904c6ecacf22d4005fb21f89bd7122d6a079f.png)\n\nThe region $D$ is the shaded quarter-disk shown above. Rewrite\n\n$$\n\\iint_D 2xy\\sqrt{x^2+y^2}\\,\\mathrm{d}A\n$$\n\nin polar coordinates. Choose the setup whose bounds describe $D$ with $r\\ge0$ and whose integrand includes the polar area factor. Do not evaluate the integral." :question/worked-solution "Use $x=r\\cos\\theta$, $y=r\\sin\\theta$, and $\\mathrm{d}A=r\\,\\mathrm{d}r\\,\\mathrm{d}\\theta$. The extra factor of $r$ comes from the area element.\n\nThe shaded region lies in the second quadrant inside the circle of radius $2$. Its boundary rays are the positive $y$-axis and negative $x$-axis, so\n\n$$\n0\\le r\\le2,\\qquad \\frac{\\pi}{2}\\le\\theta\\le\\pi\n$$\n\nUse $\\cos^2\\theta+\\sin^2\\theta=1$ and $\\sqrt{r^2}=r$ because $r\\ge0$. Then use $2\\sin\\theta\\cos\\theta=\\sin(2\\theta)$:\n\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2xy\\sqrt{x^2+y^2}&=2(r\\cos\\theta)(r\\sin\\theta)r\\\\&=2r^3\\sin\\theta\\cos\\theta\\\\&=r^3\\sin(2\\theta).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nMultiplying by the area factor $r$ gives $r^4\\sin(2\\theta)\\,\\mathrm{d}r\\,\\mathrm{d}\\theta$.\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\iint_D 2xy\\sqrt{x^2+y^2}\\,\\mathrm{d}A=\\displaystyle\\int_{\\pi/2}^{\\pi}\\int_{0}^{2}r^4\\sin(2\\theta)\\,\\mathrm{d}r\\,\\mathrm{d}\\theta.\n$$\n\n"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186706902 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/feedback "Used the squared radius as the radial bound." :answer/id #uuid "23017726-9587-5148-a58f-ea46cc24d3ea" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\displaystyle\\int_{-\\pi/2}^{0}\\int_{0}^{4}r^3\\sin(2\\theta)\\,\\mathrm{d}r\\,\\mathrm{d}\\theta" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186706885} {:answer/feedback "Omitted the Jacobian factor $r$." :answer/id #uuid "2b4f6bd3-065c-5369-9a78-c734c212a01c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\displaystyle\\int_{-\\pi/2}^{0}\\int_{0}^{2}r^2\\sin(2\\theta)\\,\\mathrm{d}r\\,\\mathrm{d}\\theta" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186706888} {:answer/feedback "The bounds, substituted integrand, and polar area factor are all correct." :answer/id #uuid "3c475e68-6fa5-5ebc-9ce9-e36b438d2d94" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\displaystyle\\int_{-\\pi/2}^{0}\\int_{0}^{2}r^3\\sin(2\\theta)\\,\\mathrm{d}r\\,\\mathrm{d}\\theta" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186706889} {:answer/feedback "These angles describe the first quadrant." :answer/id #uuid "6a49f600-f55b-5dc6-a99c-8c0b938d9038" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\displaystyle\\int_{0}^{\\pi/2}\\int_{0}^{2}r^3\\sin(2\\theta)\\,\\mathrm{d}r\\,\\mathrm{d}\\theta" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186706891} {:answer/feedback "Lost the factor $2$: $2\\sin\\theta\\cos\\theta=\\sin(2\\theta)$." :answer/id #uuid "bee3bfe3-2268-52cd-9518-bf348df0cc09" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\displaystyle\\int_{-\\pi/2}^{0}\\int_{0}^{2}r^3\\sin\\theta\\cos\\theta\\,\\mathrm{d}r\\,\\mathrm{d}\\theta" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186706898}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/feedback "The bounds, substituted integrand, and polar area factor are all correct." :answer/id #uuid "3c475e68-6fa5-5ebc-9ce9-e36b438d2d94" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\displaystyle\\int_{-\\pi/2}^{0}\\int_{0}^{2}r^3\\sin(2\\theta)\\,\\mathrm{d}r\\,\\mathrm{d}\\theta" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186706889} :answer-field/id #uuid "29d93ae5-dd59-5b3d-98c8-a12dc5026c59" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186706887}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "fe5b4db3-bac6-55e0-aac3-c0dc7dc18f6b" :question/problem "![Shaded quarter-disk centered at the origin](images/b4/b4c3a67f9864049dc09e243365c0882f06488ef46075464eec141215c470c6e8.png)\n\nThe region $D$ is the shaded quarter-disk shown above. Rewrite\n\n$$\n\\iint_D 2xy\\,\\mathrm{d}A\n$$\n\nin polar coordinates. Choose the setup whose bounds describe $D$ with $r\\ge0$ and whose integrand includes the polar area factor. Do not evaluate the integral." :question/worked-solution "Use $x=r\\cos\\theta$, $y=r\\sin\\theta$, and $\\mathrm{d}A=r\\,\\mathrm{d}r\\,\\mathrm{d}\\theta$. The extra factor of $r$ comes from the area element.\n\nThe shaded region lies in the fourth quadrant inside the circle of radius $2$. Starting on the negative $y$-axis and turning counterclockwise to the positive $x$-axis gives\n\n$$\n0\\le r\\le2,\\qquad -\\frac{\\pi}{2}\\le\\theta\\le0\n$$\n\nSubstitute and use $2\\sin\\theta\\cos\\theta=\\sin(2\\theta)$:\n\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2xy\\,\\mathrm{d}A&=2(r\\cos\\theta)(r\\sin\\theta)r\\,\\mathrm{d}r\\,\\mathrm{d}\\theta\\\\&=2r^3\\sin\\theta\\cos\\theta\\,\\mathrm{d}r\\,\\mathrm{d}\\theta\\\\&=r^3\\sin(2\\theta)\\,\\mathrm{d}r\\,\\mathrm{d}\\theta.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\iint_D 2xy\\,\\mathrm{d}A=\\displaystyle\\int_{-\\pi/2}^{0}\\int_{0}^{2}r^3\\sin(2\\theta)\\,\\mathrm{d}r\\,\\mathrm{d}\\theta.\n$$\n\n"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186707146 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "09d3fe42-617a-573e-8256-b7b54dddd6da" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-12" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186707144} {:answer/id #uuid "402524b3-02af-57bf-81f9-85a8a17dfd64" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186707150} {:answer/id #uuid "b883197d-fdcc-58a5-a5d0-cc6450d2e1c9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-8" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186707158} {:answer/id #uuid "deb817e9-d19e-567d-a1cc-40337e58f5a0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "48" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186707167} {:answer/id #uuid "ea3c3bd4-1cf9-5ee3-9417-3d2bc2bfe707" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "- \\frac{3}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186707174}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "09d3fe42-617a-573e-8256-b7b54dddd6da" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-12" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186707144} :answer-field/id #uuid "bcf5a65d-9d36-5aa1-8d68-1065f45d41b3" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186707159}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "13a86bec-0b84-591c-82f2-f5db6466854c" :question/problem "Evaluate $\\iint_D - 3 y \\sqrt{x^{2} + y^{2}}\\,dA$, where $D$ is the portion of the disk $x^2+y^2\\le 4$ where $x\\ge0,\\ y\\ge0$." :question/worked-solution "Use $x=r\\cos\\theta$, $y=r\\sin\\theta$ and $dA=r\\,dr\\,d\\theta$, with $r\\ge0$.\n\nThe Cartesian restrictions give the following polar region.\n\n![](images/d9/d919a1a51a86933bbe207c280000d3cb2a0af687445ad4f8a327db339715fed7.png)\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}0\\le r&\\le 2 \\\\\n0\\le\\theta&\\le \\frac{\\pi}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\nHere $x^2+y^2=r^2(\\cos^2\\theta+\\sin^2\\theta)=r^2$. Since $r\\ge0$, $\\sqrt{x^2+y^2}=r$.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\iint_D - 3 y \\sqrt{x^{2} + y^{2}}\\,dA&=\\int_{0}^{\\frac{\\pi}{2}}\\int_0^{2}- 3 r^{3} \\sin{\\left(\\theta \\right)}\\,dr\\,d\\theta \\\\\n&=\\int_{0}^{\\frac{\\pi}{2}}\\left[- \\frac{3 r^{4} \\sin{\\left(\\theta \\right)}}{4}\\right]_0^{2}\\,d\\theta \\\\\n&=\\int_{0}^{\\frac{\\pi}{2}}\\left(- 12 \\sin{\\left(\\theta \\right)}\\right)\\,d\\theta \\\\\n&=\\left[12 \\cos{\\left(\\theta \\right)}\\right]_{0}^{\\frac{\\pi}{2}} \\\\\n&=\\left(0\\right)-\\left(12\\right) \\\\\n&=-12\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186707148 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "e2adb339-63a0-58fe-8ce3-f85a07ceeef2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-9" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186707168} {:answer/id #uuid "e586f818-5484-5c16-bfc0-99a7f9f3e705" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-18" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186707169} {:answer/id #uuid "e6b09a80-58f3-50ca-9c02-d1f7360813ef" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-81" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186707170} {:answer/id #uuid "e7e5b338-9cdd-5872-aa81-b350b453ff04" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186707172} {:answer/id #uuid "f83d2088-98f6-55f5-9c1b-b5f93916174d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-27" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186707177}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "f83d2088-98f6-55f5-9c1b-b5f93916174d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-27" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186707177} :answer-field/id #uuid "9ef32172-b166-5118-996c-8b182d490d84" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186707153}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "1fd35840-1521-5928-a7bd-edd9c10ae1cb" :question/problem "Evaluate $\\iint_D x + 2 y\\,dA$, where $D$ is the portion of the disk $x^2+y^2\\le 9$ where $x\\le0,\\ y\\le0$." :question/worked-solution "Use $x=r\\cos\\theta$, $y=r\\sin\\theta$ and $dA=r\\,dr\\,d\\theta$, with $r\\ge0$.\n\nThe Cartesian restrictions give the following polar region.\n\n![](images/d0/d04f034896058356f7d978567dad992206e558d0cadad1f56ba3ab430d1b147e.png)\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}0\\le r&\\le 3 \\\\\n\\pi\\le\\theta&\\le \\frac{3 \\pi}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\iint_D x + 2 y\\,dA&=\\int_{\\pi}^{\\frac{3 \\pi}{2}}\\int_0^{3}r^{2} \\left(2 \\sin{\\left(\\theta \\right)} + \\cos{\\left(\\theta \\right)}\\right)\\,dr\\,d\\theta \\\\\n&=\\int_{\\pi}^{\\frac{3 \\pi}{2}}\\left[\\frac{r^{3} \\left(2 \\sin{\\left(\\theta \\right)} + \\cos{\\left(\\theta \\right)}\\right)}{3}\\right]_0^{3}\\,d\\theta \\\\\n&=\\int_{\\pi}^{\\frac{3 \\pi}{2}}\\left(9 \\left(2 \\sin{\\left(\\theta \\right)} + \\cos{\\left(\\theta \\right)}\\right)\\right)\\,d\\theta \\\\\n&=\\left[9 \\sin{\\left(\\theta \\right)} - 18 \\cos{\\left(\\theta \\right)}\\right]_{\\pi}^{\\frac{3 \\pi}{2}} \\\\\n&=\\left(-9\\right)-\\left(18\\right) \\\\\n&=-27\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186707149 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0b8978b6-0418-56c8-8445-205ca2337071" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "- \\frac{1}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186707145} {:answer/id #uuid "178e4a31-5f72-546a-87a5-ced4a7adcbe8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-8" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186707147} {:answer/id #uuid "8cb588bb-1765-5652-8e0f-5f9bc50d6c2f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186707152} {:answer/id #uuid "c0dfe04d-fd26-5766-92cf-8dbc4c738666" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186707160} {:answer/id #uuid "c4e7e9ae-2e8c-5dbc-874f-6b1417136e35" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "16" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186707162}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "c0dfe04d-fd26-5766-92cf-8dbc4c738666" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186707160} :answer-field/id #uuid "dc81b6b3-2187-5159-8b30-dcb13ce857ed" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186707165}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "3eb8cbaa-d967-59ca-9b7d-59b6563688fb" :question/problem "Evaluate $\\iint_D - y \\sqrt{x^{2} + y^{2}}\\,dA$, where $D$ is the portion of the disk $x^2+y^2\\le 4$ where $x\\ge0,\\ y\\le0$." :question/worked-solution "Use $x=r\\cos\\theta$, $y=r\\sin\\theta$ and $dA=r\\,dr\\,d\\theta$, with $r\\ge0$.\n\nThe Cartesian restrictions give the following polar region.\n\n![](images/cf/cfcf5a05620442b1c529e896e6018540a976d1349c3c942f8b027ef6f757ae7f.png)\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}0\\le r&\\le 2 \\\\\n- \\frac{\\pi}{2}\\le\\theta&\\le 0\\end{aligned}\n$$\nHere $x^2+y^2=r^2(\\cos^2\\theta+\\sin^2\\theta)=r^2$. Since $r\\ge0$, $\\sqrt{x^2+y^2}=r$.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\iint_D - y \\sqrt{x^{2} + y^{2}}\\,dA&=\\int_{- \\frac{\\pi}{2}}^{0}\\int_0^{2}- r^{3} \\sin{\\left(\\theta \\right)}\\,dr\\,d\\theta \\\\\n&=\\int_{- \\frac{\\pi}{2}}^{0}\\left[- \\frac{r^{4} \\sin{\\left(\\theta \\right)}}{4}\\right]_0^{2}\\,d\\theta \\\\\n&=\\int_{- \\frac{\\pi}{2}}^{0}\\left(- 4 \\sin{\\left(\\theta \\right)}\\right)\\,d\\theta \\\\\n&=\\left[4 \\cos{\\left(\\theta \\right)}\\right]_{- \\frac{\\pi}{2}}^{0} \\\\\n&=\\left(4\\right)-\\left(0\\right) \\\\\n&=4\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186707163 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "06415642-22b6-5854-a375-f1d26c976885" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3 \\pi \\ln{\\left(\\frac{13}{4} \\right)}}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186707143} {:answer/id #uuid "864cf11f-7152-5ce2-bbd0-0bbefe38d63a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3 \\pi \\ln{\\left(10 \\right)}}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186707151} {:answer/id #uuid "a46f44a7-cc05-59d8-b95c-df37d1f1189c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "- \\frac{3 \\pi \\ln{\\left(10 \\right)}}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186707155} {:answer/id #uuid "a9418ee6-bdb7-5c61-a848-0a5384552abd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "- \\frac{3 \\pi \\ln{\\left(\\frac{13}{4} \\right)}}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186707156} {:answer/id #uuid "e6fcffa7-c049-5fc6-af8c-a90a1a216d68" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3 \\pi \\ln{\\left(10 \\right)}}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186707171}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "e6fcffa7-c049-5fc6-af8c-a90a1a216d68" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3 \\pi \\ln{\\left(10 \\right)}}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186707171} :answer-field/id #uuid "aff82793-01f7-5ddf-9c61-ebb4fab99e33" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186707157}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "c4fff9ef-f524-5cde-b212-eb1e587dd57e" :question/problem "Evaluate $\\iint_D \\frac{3}{x^{2} + y^{2} + 1}\\,dA$, where $D$ is the portion of the disk $x^2+y^2\\le 9$ where $x\\le0,\\ y\\le0$." :question/worked-solution "Use $x=r\\cos\\theta$, $y=r\\sin\\theta$ and $dA=r\\,dr\\,d\\theta$, with $r\\ge0$.\n\nThe Cartesian restrictions give the following polar region.\n\n![](images/d0/d04f034896058356f7d978567dad992206e558d0cadad1f56ba3ab430d1b147e.png)\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}0\\le r&\\le 3 \\\\\n\\pi\\le\\theta&\\le \\frac{3 \\pi}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\nHere $x^2+y^2=r^2(\\cos^2\\theta+\\sin^2\\theta)=r^2$.\nFor the radial primitive, set $u=r^2+1$, so $du=2r\\,dr$; its logarithm is defined since $r^2+1>0$.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\iint_D \\frac{3}{x^{2} + y^{2} + 1}\\,dA&=\\int_{\\pi}^{\\frac{3 \\pi}{2}}\\int_0^{3}\\frac{3 r}{r^{2} + 1}\\,dr\\,d\\theta \\\\\n&=\\int_{\\pi}^{\\frac{3 \\pi}{2}}\\left[\\frac{3 \\ln{\\left(r^{2} + 1 \\right)}}{2}\\right]_0^{3}\\,d\\theta \\\\\n&=\\int_{\\pi}^{\\frac{3 \\pi}{2}}\\left(\\frac{3 \\ln{\\left(10 \\right)}}{2}\\right)\\,d\\theta \\\\\n&=\\left[\\frac{3 \\theta \\ln{\\left(10 \\right)}}{2}\\right]_{\\pi}^{\\frac{3 \\pi}{2}} \\\\\n&=\\left(\\frac{9 \\pi \\ln{\\left(10 \\right)}}{4}\\right)-\\left(\\frac{3 \\pi \\ln{\\left(10 \\right)}}{2}\\right) \\\\\n&=\\frac{3 \\pi \\ln{\\left(10 \\right)}}{4}\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186707164 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "a37999f4-ce84-5542-862a-d4679cff49b9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "- \\frac{\\pi}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186707154} {:answer/id #uuid "c48a173f-87ff-5825-bf08-1bd747617c5b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\pi" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186707161} {:answer/id #uuid "e7f25bb2-93d0-5fa4-a348-b748284143db" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "- \\frac{\\pi}{16}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186707173} {:answer/id #uuid "f0c4e804-daf2-5750-a47b-5d4c5f25c642" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "- \\pi" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186707175} {:answer/id #uuid "f3eab12f-0e75-5ef5-aaaf-c9a2863c7857" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "- \\frac{\\pi}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186707176}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "a37999f4-ce84-5542-862a-d4679cff49b9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "- \\frac{\\pi}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186707154} :answer-field/id #uuid "ddd27ec8-40b2-5a92-967b-27bcc0768e5b" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186707166}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "cf1f5562-b0fd-5986-9916-d0bd9456c231" :question/problem "Evaluate $\\iint_D - x^{2} - y^{2}\\,dA$, where $D$ is the portion of the disk $x^2+y^2\\le 1$ where $y\\le0$." :question/worked-solution "Use $x=r\\cos\\theta$, $y=r\\sin\\theta$ and $dA=r\\,dr\\,d\\theta$, with $r\\ge0$.\n\nThe Cartesian restrictions give the following polar region.\n\n![](images/33/335dde7dc67f1b7f582c8e2f2d40e01271ce83d0f184fc86211c1171d244897e.png)\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}0\\le r&\\le 1 \\\\\n\\pi\\le\\theta&\\le 2 \\pi\\end{aligned}\n$$\nHere $x^2+y^2=r^2(\\cos^2\\theta+\\sin^2\\theta)=r^2$.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\iint_D - x^{2} - y^{2}\\,dA&=\\int_{\\pi}^{2 \\pi}\\int_0^{1}- r^{3}\\,dr\\,d\\theta \\\\\n&=\\int_{\\pi}^{2 \\pi}\\left[- \\frac{r^{4}}{4}\\right]_0^{1}\\,d\\theta \\\\\n&=\\int_{\\pi}^{2 \\pi}\\left(- \\frac{1}{4}\\right)\\,d\\theta \\\\\n&=\\left[- \\frac{\\theta}{4}\\right]_{\\pi}^{2 \\pi} \\\\\n&=\\left(- \\frac{\\pi}{2}\\right)-\\left(- \\frac{\\pi}{4}\\right) \\\\\n&=- \\frac{\\pi}{4}\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186707194 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2e0a7eb8-32f0-5041-bb4b-b64e54593884" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{9}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186707182} {:answer/id #uuid "3553c520-4652-599f-969a-4b9a7c34ad9a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{9}{16}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186707184} {:answer/id #uuid "3f9aa42b-b8d7-5914-b75c-af2a59e809e8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "- \\frac{9}{16}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186707186} {:answer/id #uuid "47b7a37b-9a44-59c1-8da8-0f2afbba1c12" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "9" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186707187} {:answer/id #uuid "eac708bf-09aa-5a6b-af4f-4788bb3c4ec7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186707198}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "2e0a7eb8-32f0-5041-bb4b-b64e54593884" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{9}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186707182} :answer-field/id #uuid "c3780e86-c2bf-59f4-8037-6aff0a1e2e94" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186707195}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "b556547f-15db-5e0c-9db7-2706330ceabd" :question/problem "Evaluate $\\iint_D - \\frac{y}{x}\\,dA$, where $D$ is the part of the disk where $y\\ge0$ bounded by $x^2+y^2=- 3 x$, shown below.\n\n![](images/eb/eb6ab6771940a63f6c826f7fef79305115b2f83f894e54c6d60f038982000833.png)" :question/worked-solution "Use $x=r\\cos\\theta$, $y=r\\sin\\theta$ and $dA=r\\,dr\\,d\\theta$, with $r\\ge0$.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x^2+y^2&=-3x \\\\\nr^2&=-3r\\cos\\theta \\\\\nr&=- 3 \\cos{\\left(\\theta \\right)}\\quad(r>0)\\end{aligned}\n$$\nThe selected part of the circle gives the angular interval below; the origin is included at the radial lower bound.\n\n![](images/eb/eb6ab6771940a63f6c826f7fef79305115b2f83f894e54c6d60f038982000833.png)\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}0\\le r&\\le - 3 \\cos{\\left(\\theta \\right)} \\\\\n\\frac{\\pi}{2}\\le\\theta&\\le \\pi\\end{aligned}\n$$\nThe denominator vanishes only at boundary endpoints. Interpret angular endpoints as one-sided limits. Radial evaluation cancels the apparent singularity, leaving the continuous angular expression below, so the improper integral converges.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\iint_D - \\frac{y}{x}\\,dA&=\\int_{\\frac{\\pi}{2}}^{\\pi}\\int_0^{- 3 \\cos{\\left(\\theta \\right)}}- \\frac{r \\sin{\\left(\\theta \\right)}}{\\cos{\\left(\\theta \\right)}}\\,dr\\,d\\theta \\\\\n&=\\int_{\\frac{\\pi}{2}}^{\\pi}\\left[- \\frac{r^{2} \\sin{\\left(\\theta \\right)}}{2 \\cos{\\left(\\theta \\right)}}\\right]_0^{- 3 \\cos{\\left(\\theta \\right)}}\\,d\\theta \\\\\n&=\\int_{\\frac{\\pi}{2}}^{\\pi}\\left(- \\frac{9 \\sin{\\left(\\theta \\right)} \\cos{\\left(\\theta \\right)}}{2}\\right)\\,d\\theta \\\\\n&=\\left[\\frac{9 \\cos^{2}{\\left(\\theta \\right)}}{4}\\right]_{\\frac{\\pi}{2}}^{\\pi} \\\\\n&=\\left(\\frac{9}{4}\\right)-\\left(0\\right) \\\\\n&=\\frac{9}{4}\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186707196 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2fc1805f-ab7c-5de0-9967-175788955ea5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186707183} {:answer/id #uuid "3dba34c0-bda2-5e04-88ab-4b3e338b824c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186707185} {:answer/id #uuid "6cbcbeed-4dcb-520c-a974-c5a90d8214e8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "8" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186707189} {:answer/id #uuid "94c62ce8-c32a-529c-9d47-803982086de6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186707190} {:answer/id #uuid "ea350927-7f02-5598-a04f-29278ccee18c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186707197}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "2fc1805f-ab7c-5de0-9967-175788955ea5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186707183} :answer-field/id #uuid "0090c804-a233-5571-bc16-246016ab3ca1" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186707179}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "c52223fb-4239-5990-b323-1745fb1866b1" :question/problem "Evaluate $\\iint_D \\frac{2 y}{x}\\,dA$, where $D$ is the part of the disk where $y\\ge0$ bounded by $x^2+y^2=- 2 x$, shown below.\n\n![](images/f6/f6c8c5af2bfe7fe3579510bceabdfdb9c40ed413eaba6a3aaaf9e90c30eb9b62.png)" :question/worked-solution "Use $x=r\\cos\\theta$, $y=r\\sin\\theta$ and $dA=r\\,dr\\,d\\theta$, with $r\\ge0$.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x^2+y^2&=-2x \\\\\nr^2&=-2r\\cos\\theta \\\\\nr&=- 2 \\cos{\\left(\\theta \\right)}\\quad(r>0)\\end{aligned}\n$$\nThe selected part of the circle gives the angular interval below; the origin is included at the radial lower bound.\n\n![](images/f6/f6c8c5af2bfe7fe3579510bceabdfdb9c40ed413eaba6a3aaaf9e90c30eb9b62.png)\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}0\\le r&\\le - 2 \\cos{\\left(\\theta \\right)} \\\\\n\\frac{\\pi}{2}\\le\\theta&\\le \\pi\\end{aligned}\n$$\nThe denominator vanishes only at boundary endpoints. Interpret angular endpoints as one-sided limits. Radial evaluation cancels the apparent singularity, leaving the continuous angular expression below, so the improper integral converges.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\iint_D \\frac{2 y}{x}\\,dA&=\\int_{\\frac{\\pi}{2}}^{\\pi}\\int_0^{- 2 \\cos{\\left(\\theta \\right)}}\\frac{2 r \\sin{\\left(\\theta \\right)}}{\\cos{\\left(\\theta \\right)}}\\,dr\\,d\\theta \\\\\n&=\\int_{\\frac{\\pi}{2}}^{\\pi}\\left[\\frac{r^{2} \\sin{\\left(\\theta \\right)}}{\\cos{\\left(\\theta \\right)}}\\right]_0^{- 2 \\cos{\\left(\\theta \\right)}}\\,d\\theta \\\\\n&=\\int_{\\frac{\\pi}{2}}^{\\pi}\\left(4 \\sin{\\left(\\theta \\right)} \\cos{\\left(\\theta \\right)}\\right)\\,d\\theta \\\\\n&=\\left[- 2 \\cos^{2}{\\left(\\theta \\right)}\\right]_{\\frac{\\pi}{2}}^{\\pi} \\\\\n&=\\left(-2\\right)-\\left(0\\right) \\\\\n&=-2\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186707199 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "274f7a57-c4a9-5eaf-9b99-58066f74c69a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{972}{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186707181} {:answer/id #uuid "4b67d115-1e6f-54ae-a834-f9a3ccd6caa5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "- \\frac{243}{20}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186707188} {:answer/id #uuid "993ea450-44ae-5f82-9672-db44727c5ea1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "- \\frac{243}{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186707191} {:answer/id #uuid "a874293e-28c1-5318-a148-7b199a2d4d60" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "- \\frac{972}{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186707192} {:answer/id #uuid "a9eca66c-fee2-5434-868c-e8d1d8904c1b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{243}{320}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186707193}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "4b67d115-1e6f-54ae-a834-f9a3ccd6caa5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "- \\frac{243}{20}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186707188} :answer-field/id #uuid "105375d9-8a59-55a5-b1f4-0c9556d65470" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186707180}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "ec1914cb-c72a-57e4-9775-b5461c461baa" :question/problem "Evaluate $\\iint_D 3 x \\sqrt{x^{2} + y^{2}}\\,dA$, where $D$ is the part of the disk where $x\\le0$ bounded by $x^2+y^2=3 y$, shown below.\n\n![](images/3f/3ff147b4f3ab0dc7db522a812b6d8d7505506a28b5dc31d33a562184240c721e.png)" :question/worked-solution "Use $x=r\\cos\\theta$, $y=r\\sin\\theta$ and $dA=r\\,dr\\,d\\theta$, with $r\\ge0$.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x^2+y^2&=3y \\\\\nr^2&=3r\\sin\\theta \\\\\nr&=3 \\sin{\\left(\\theta \\right)}\\quad(r>0)\\end{aligned}\n$$\nThe selected part of the circle gives the angular interval below; the origin is included at the radial lower bound.\n\n![](images/3f/3ff147b4f3ab0dc7db522a812b6d8d7505506a28b5dc31d33a562184240c721e.png)\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}0\\le r&\\le 3 \\sin{\\left(\\theta \\right)} \\\\\n\\frac{\\pi}{2}\\le\\theta&\\le \\pi\\end{aligned}\n$$\nHere $x^2+y^2=r^2(\\cos^2\\theta+\\sin^2\\theta)=r^2$. Since $r\\ge0$, $\\sqrt{x^2+y^2}=r$.\nFor the angular primitive use $u=\\sin\\theta$, $du=\\cos\\theta\\,d\\theta$.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\iint_D 3 x \\sqrt{x^{2} + y^{2}}\\,dA&=\\int_{\\frac{\\pi}{2}}^{\\pi}\\int_0^{3 \\sin{\\left(\\theta \\right)}}3 r^{3} \\cos{\\left(\\theta \\right)}\\,dr\\,d\\theta \\\\\n&=\\int_{\\frac{\\pi}{2}}^{\\pi}\\left[\\frac{3 r^{4} \\cos{\\left(\\theta \\right)}}{4}\\right]_0^{3 \\sin{\\left(\\theta \\right)}}\\,d\\theta \\\\\n&=\\int_{\\frac{\\pi}{2}}^{\\pi}\\left(\\frac{243 \\sin^{4}{\\left(\\theta \\right)} \\cos{\\left(\\theta \\right)}}{4}\\right)\\,d\\theta \\\\\n&=\\left[\\frac{243 \\sin^{5}{\\left(\\theta \\right)}}{20}\\right]_{\\frac{\\pi}{2}}^{\\pi} \\\\\n&=\\left(0\\right)-\\left(\\frac{243}{20}\\right) \\\\\n&=- \\frac{243}{20}\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186707203 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "455fd94f-078a-5487-b793-a7250aafb66e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{2 \\pi}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186707201} {:answer/id #uuid "cea2dab6-e4b8-523b-9826-4cc5f5a4ec33" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "- \\frac{\\pi}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186707202} {:answer/id #uuid "f2ccf8dc-35ba-553c-9f8d-d30016e7d10f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "- \\frac{2 \\pi}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186707204} {:answer/id #uuid "f57a216e-f7f9-57fd-8819-7e15f0aefe31" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\pi}{8}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186707205} {:answer/id #uuid "fdb27b83-1377-5d60-abc1-1cf2de10faa6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "- \\pi" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186707206}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "cea2dab6-e4b8-523b-9826-4cc5f5a4ec33" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "- \\frac{\\pi}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186707202} :answer-field/id #uuid "1481c56e-90a8-553f-ab6e-c3b998fd5ae4" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186707200}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "da8bd0cc-88cb-501c-b46e-e45e964bc8a4" :question/problem "Evaluate $\\iint_D - x^{2} - y^{2}\\,dA$, where $D$ is the disk $x^2+y^2\\le 1$." :question/worked-solution "Use $x=r\\cos\\theta$, $y=r\\sin\\theta$ and $dA=r\\,dr\\,d\\theta$, with $r\\ge0$.\n\nThe Cartesian restrictions give the following polar region.\n\n![](images/4d/4d9d33bee82c23fc4e77f2f665931c7617f07fb5aa3ddbd0f8d09f3451fceecb.png)\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}0\\le r&\\le 1 \\\\\n0\\le\\theta&\\le 2 \\pi\\end{aligned}\n$$\nHere $x^2+y^2=r^2(\\cos^2\\theta+\\sin^2\\theta)=r^2$.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\iint_D - x^{2} - y^{2}\\,dA&=\\int_{0}^{2 \\pi}\\int_0^{1}- r^{3}\\,dr\\,d\\theta \\\\\n&=\\int_{0}^{2 \\pi}\\left[- \\frac{r^{4}}{4}\\right]_0^{1}\\,d\\theta \\\\\n&=\\int_{0}^{2 \\pi}\\left(- \\frac{1}{4}\\right)\\,d\\theta \\\\\n&=\\left[- \\frac{\\theta}{4}\\right]_{0}^{2 \\pi} \\\\\n&=\\left(- \\frac{\\pi}{2}\\right)-\\left(0\\right) \\\\\n&=- \\frac{\\pi}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186707230 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "6300415b-df5d-520a-80af-47c4f4fb9575" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\displaystyle\\int_{0}^{\\pi}\\int_{0}^{\\sin{\\left(\\theta \\right)}}r^{5} \\sin^{2}{\\left(\\theta \\right)} \\cos{\\left(\\theta \\right)}\\,\\mathrm{d}r\\,\\mathrm{d}\\theta" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186707239} {:answer/id #uuid "85aac80b-0592-5169-b5f8-442f1cfc1e34" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\displaystyle\\int_{0}^{\\pi}\\int_{0}^{\\frac{1}{2}}r^{3} \\sin^{2}{\\left(\\theta \\right)} \\cos{\\left(\\theta \\right)}\\,\\mathrm{d}r\\,\\mathrm{d}\\theta" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186707245} {:answer/id #uuid "92b7b410-e194-5dea-b875-e93ea48557ee" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\displaystyle\\int_{\\frac{\\pi}{2}}^{\\frac{3 \\pi}{2}}\\int_{0}^{- \\cos{\\left(\\theta \\right)}}r^{3} \\sin^{2}{\\left(\\theta \\right)} \\cos{\\left(\\theta \\right)}\\,\\mathrm{d}r\\,\\mathrm{d}\\theta" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186707249} {:answer/id #uuid "aca8069b-c814-508e-84e6-2ce3d7de5371" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\displaystyle\\int_{0}^{\\pi}\\int_{0}^{\\frac{1}{2}}r^{4} \\sin^{2}{\\left(\\theta \\right)} \\cos{\\left(\\theta \\right)}\\,\\mathrm{d}r\\,\\mathrm{d}\\theta" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186707252} {:answer/id #uuid "c3d465f7-45b6-5279-9486-7060448da862" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\displaystyle\\int_{0}^{\\pi}\\int_{0}^{\\sin{\\left(\\theta \\right)}}r^{4} \\sin^{2}{\\left(\\theta \\right)} \\cos{\\left(\\theta \\right)}\\,\\mathrm{d}r\\,\\mathrm{d}\\theta" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186707254}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "c3d465f7-45b6-5279-9486-7060448da862" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\displaystyle\\int_{0}^{\\pi}\\int_{0}^{\\sin{\\left(\\theta \\right)}}r^{4} \\sin^{2}{\\left(\\theta \\right)} \\cos{\\left(\\theta \\right)}\\,\\mathrm{d}r\\,\\mathrm{d}\\theta" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186707254} :answer-field/id #uuid "0d20398a-4cb8-570f-826f-01b6be290d1f" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186707231}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "01bf462a-11ba-5f3b-9c84-759ffcb8189e" :question/problem "Let $D=\\{(x,y):x^{2}+(y-\\frac{1}{2})^{2}\\leq\\frac{1}{4}\\}$. Rewrite\n\n$$\n\\iint_D x y^{2}\\,\\mathrm{d}A.\n$$\n\nSelect the polar-coordinate setup whose bounds cover $D$ once with $r\\geq0$ and whose integrand includes the area factor. Do not evaluate the integral." :question/worked-solution "Use $x=r\\cos\\theta$, $y=r\\sin\\theta$, and $\\mathrm{d}A=r\\,\\mathrm{d}r\\,\\mathrm{d}\\theta$. The full disk has radius $\\frac{1}{2}$ and center $(0,\\frac{1}{2})$.\n\n**Radial bounds.** Expanding the disk inequality gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x^2+y^2-1y&\\leq0\\\\r^2-1r\\sin{\\left(\\theta \\right)}&\\leq0\\\\r(r-(\\sin{\\left(\\theta \\right)}))&\\leq0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFor $r>0$, divide by $r$; the origin is included as well. Thus $0\\leq r\\leq \\sin{\\left(\\theta \\right)}$.\n\n**Angular bounds.** The upper radial bound must be nonnegative. The full disk permits $0\\leq\\theta\\leq\\pi$. Choose $0\\leq\\theta\\leq\\pi$. Every interior point is covered once.\n\n![Region, representative radial segment and angular sweep](images/4c/4c9c4c04113e0e8c940bca28430d29116ca0273b7523a293bc53560e42d11f4b.png)\n\n**Transformed integrand.** The density substitution and area factor give\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x y^{2}&=r^{3} \\sin^{2}{\\left(\\theta \\right)} \\cos{\\left(\\theta \\right)}\\\\x y^{2}\\,\\mathrm{d}A&=r^{4} \\sin^{2}{\\left(\\theta \\right)} \\cos{\\left(\\theta \\right)}\\,\\mathrm{d}r\\,\\mathrm{d}\\theta\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore the required setup is\n\n$$\n\\displaystyle\\int_{0}^{\\pi}\\int_{0}^{\\sin{\\left(\\theta \\right)}}r^{4} \\sin^{2}{\\left(\\theta \\right)} \\cos{\\left(\\theta \\right)}\\,\\mathrm{d}r\\,\\mathrm{d}\\theta.\n$$\n\n**Other choices.** $\\displaystyle\\int_{0}^{\\pi}\\int_{0}^{\\sin{\\left(\\theta \\right)}}r^{5} \\sin^{2}{\\left(\\theta \\right)} \\cos{\\left(\\theta \\right)}\\,\\mathrm{d}r\\,\\mathrm{d}\\theta$: The area factor was squared instead of used once. $\\displaystyle\\int_{\\frac{\\pi}{2}}^{\\frac{3 \\pi}{2}}\\int_{0}^{- \\cos{\\left(\\theta \\right)}}r^{3} \\sin^{2}{\\left(\\theta \\right)} \\cos{\\left(\\theta \\right)}\\,\\mathrm{d}r\\,\\mathrm{d}\\theta$: These bounds describe a different disk or part-disk, with a different center direction. The area factor is missing. $\\displaystyle\\int_{0}^{\\pi}\\int_{0}^{\\frac{1}{2}}r^{4} \\sin^{2}{\\left(\\theta \\right)} \\cos{\\left(\\theta \\right)}\\,\\mathrm{d}r\\,\\mathrm{d}\\theta$: The center-to-boundary radius is incorrectly used as a constant polar upper bound. $\\displaystyle\\int_{0}^{\\pi}\\int_{0}^{\\frac{1}{2}}r^{3} \\sin^{2}{\\left(\\theta \\right)} \\cos{\\left(\\theta \\right)}\\,\\mathrm{d}r\\,\\mathrm{d}\\theta$: The center-to-boundary radius is incorrectly used as a constant polar upper bound. The area factor is missing."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186707240 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1eed9b97-f38d-5b90-90c6-52d1051019d4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\displaystyle\\int_{\\frac{\\pi}{2}}^{\\frac{3 \\pi}{2}}\\int_{0}^{- 4 \\cos{\\left(\\theta \\right)}}r^{3} \\cos{\\left(\\theta \\right)}\\,\\mathrm{d}r\\,\\mathrm{d}\\theta" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186707232} {:answer/id #uuid "6001baae-137d-5bfe-a3ed-48149254c0e7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\displaystyle\\int_{\\frac{\\pi}{2}}^{\\frac{3 \\pi}{2}}\\int_{0}^{- 4 \\cos{\\left(\\theta \\right)}}r^{2} \\cos{\\left(\\theta \\right)}\\,\\mathrm{d}r\\,\\mathrm{d}\\theta" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186707238} {:answer/id #uuid "709863ab-900b-56cd-afbb-d3398d3a0cba" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\displaystyle\\int_{\\pi}^{2 \\pi}\\int_{0}^{- 4 \\sin{\\left(\\theta \\right)}}r \\cos{\\left(\\theta \\right)}\\,\\mathrm{d}r\\,\\mathrm{d}\\theta" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186707241} {:answer/id #uuid "8038675e-946f-55da-b531-e44c9386baef" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\displaystyle\\int_{\\pi}^{2 \\pi}\\int_{0}^{- 4 \\sin{\\left(\\theta \\right)}}r^{3} \\cos{\\left(\\theta \\right)}\\,\\mathrm{d}r\\,\\mathrm{d}\\theta" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186707243} {:answer/id #uuid "875ae808-585f-5ef8-8a94-95b2fd89eb5d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\displaystyle\\int_{\\frac{\\pi}{2}}^{\\frac{3 \\pi}{2}}\\int_{0}^{2}r^{2} \\cos{\\left(\\theta \\right)}\\,\\mathrm{d}r\\,\\mathrm{d}\\theta" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186707246}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "6001baae-137d-5bfe-a3ed-48149254c0e7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\displaystyle\\int_{\\frac{\\pi}{2}}^{\\frac{3 \\pi}{2}}\\int_{0}^{- 4 \\cos{\\left(\\theta \\right)}}r^{2} \\cos{\\left(\\theta \\right)}\\,\\mathrm{d}r\\,\\mathrm{d}\\theta" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186707238} :answer-field/id #uuid "00bb0abb-b772-5d8d-bfa7-fef35a7960ee" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186707226}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "6999da0a-c30c-5726-b199-9192d22c548d" :question/problem "Let $D=\\{(x,y):(x+2)^{2}+y^{2}\\leq4\\}$. Rewrite\n\n$$\n\\iint_D x\\,\\mathrm{d}A.\n$$\n\nSelect the polar-coordinate setup whose bounds cover $D$ once with $r\\geq0$ and whose integrand includes the area factor. Do not evaluate the integral." :question/worked-solution "Use $x=r\\cos\\theta$, $y=r\\sin\\theta$, and $\\mathrm{d}A=r\\,\\mathrm{d}r\\,\\mathrm{d}\\theta$. The full disk has radius $2$ and center $(-2,0)$.\n\n**Radial bounds.** Expanding the disk inequality gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x^2+y^2+4x&\\leq0\\\\r^2+4r\\cos{\\left(\\theta \\right)}&\\leq0\\\\r(r-(- 4 \\cos{\\left(\\theta \\right)}))&\\leq0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFor $r>0$, divide by $r$; the origin is included as well. Thus $0\\leq r\\leq - 4 \\cos{\\left(\\theta \\right)}$.\n\n**Angular bounds.** The upper radial bound must be nonnegative. The full disk permits $\\frac{\\pi}{2}\\leq\\theta\\leq\\frac{3 \\pi}{2}$. Choose $\\frac{\\pi}{2}\\leq\\theta\\leq\\frac{3 \\pi}{2}$. Every interior point is covered once.\n\n![Region, representative radial segment and angular sweep](images/9c/9c6113354c11c94e6ab79576947f853e815bf881778fc155f4ea9255fac8ae29.png)\n\n**Transformed integrand.** The density substitution and area factor give\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x&=r \\cos{\\left(\\theta \\right)}\\\\x\\,\\mathrm{d}A&=r^{2} \\cos{\\left(\\theta \\right)}\\,\\mathrm{d}r\\,\\mathrm{d}\\theta\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore the required setup is\n\n$$\n\\displaystyle\\int_{\\frac{\\pi}{2}}^{\\frac{3 \\pi}{2}}\\int_{0}^{- 4 \\cos{\\left(\\theta \\right)}}r^{2} \\cos{\\left(\\theta \\right)}\\,\\mathrm{d}r\\,\\mathrm{d}\\theta.\n$$\n\n**Other choices.** $\\displaystyle\\int_{\\frac{\\pi}{2}}^{\\frac{3 \\pi}{2}}\\int_{0}^{- 4 \\cos{\\left(\\theta \\right)}}r^{3} \\cos{\\left(\\theta \\right)}\\,\\mathrm{d}r\\,\\mathrm{d}\\theta$: The area factor was squared instead of used once. $\\displaystyle\\int_{\\pi}^{2 \\pi}\\int_{0}^{- 4 \\sin{\\left(\\theta \\right)}}r^{3} \\cos{\\left(\\theta \\right)}\\,\\mathrm{d}r\\,\\mathrm{d}\\theta$: These bounds describe a different disk or part-disk, with a different center direction. The area factor was squared instead of used once. $\\displaystyle\\int_{\\pi}^{2 \\pi}\\int_{0}^{- 4 \\sin{\\left(\\theta \\right)}}r \\cos{\\left(\\theta \\right)}\\,\\mathrm{d}r\\,\\mathrm{d}\\theta$: These bounds describe a different disk or part-disk, with a different center direction. The area factor is missing. $\\displaystyle\\int_{\\frac{\\pi}{2}}^{\\frac{3 \\pi}{2}}\\int_{0}^{2}r^{2} \\cos{\\left(\\theta \\right)}\\,\\mathrm{d}r\\,\\mathrm{d}\\theta$: The center-to-boundary radius is incorrectly used as a constant polar upper bound."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186707248 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "013788f2-f22f-59ee-aa38-5256f270e37a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\displaystyle\\int_{\\pi}^{2 \\pi}\\int_{0}^{\\frac{3}{2}}r \\cos{\\left(2 \\theta \\right)}\\,\\mathrm{d}r\\,\\mathrm{d}\\theta" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186707229} {:answer/id #uuid "3050a952-0be4-5f11-9a6f-10b5783c865c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\displaystyle\\int_{- \\frac{\\pi}{2}}^{\\frac{\\pi}{2}}\\int_{0}^{3 \\cos{\\left(\\theta \\right)}}r^{2} \\cos{\\left(2 \\theta \\right)}\\,\\mathrm{d}r\\,\\mathrm{d}\\theta" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186707233} {:answer/id #uuid "a7bae241-f2b6-59ff-8adf-28c3ea5321a4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\displaystyle\\int_{\\pi}^{2 \\pi}\\int_{0}^{- 3 \\sin{\\left(\\theta \\right)}}r \\cos{\\left(2 \\theta \\right)}\\,\\mathrm{d}r\\,\\mathrm{d}\\theta" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186707250} {:answer/id #uuid "f14190dc-b26f-526d-86dc-9cca5016e72a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\displaystyle\\int_{\\pi}^{2 \\pi}\\int_{0}^{- 3 \\sin{\\left(\\theta \\right)}}r^{3} \\cos{\\left(2 \\theta \\right)}\\,\\mathrm{d}r\\,\\mathrm{d}\\theta" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186707257} {:answer/id #uuid "f4b60e7a-8bd1-59f1-88d7-bf345b67c5f4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\displaystyle\\int_{\\pi}^{2 \\pi}\\int_{0}^{\\frac{3}{2}}r^{3} \\cos{\\left(2 \\theta \\right)}\\,\\mathrm{d}r\\,\\mathrm{d}\\theta" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186707258}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "f14190dc-b26f-526d-86dc-9cca5016e72a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\displaystyle\\int_{\\pi}^{2 \\pi}\\int_{0}^{- 3 \\sin{\\left(\\theta \\right)}}r^{3} \\cos{\\left(2 \\theta \\right)}\\,\\mathrm{d}r\\,\\mathrm{d}\\theta" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186707257} :answer-field/id #uuid "830f000a-3a67-557f-bc33-244cadf2dbee" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186707244}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "8ffe86dc-71ff-544d-98c3-22e21773caac" :question/problem "Let $D=\\{(x,y):x^{2}+(y+\\frac{3}{2})^{2}\\leq\\frac{9}{4}\\}$. Rewrite\n\n$$\n\\iint_D x^{2} - y^{2}\\,\\mathrm{d}A.\n$$\n\nSelect the polar-coordinate setup whose bounds cover $D$ once with $r\\geq0$ and whose integrand includes the area factor. Do not evaluate the integral." :question/worked-solution "Use $x=r\\cos\\theta$, $y=r\\sin\\theta$, and $\\mathrm{d}A=r\\,\\mathrm{d}r\\,\\mathrm{d}\\theta$. The full disk has radius $\\frac{3}{2}$ and center $(0,- \\frac{3}{2})$.\n\n**Radial bounds.** Expanding the disk inequality gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x^2+y^2+3y&\\leq0\\\\r^2+3r\\sin{\\left(\\theta \\right)}&\\leq0\\\\r(r-(- 3 \\sin{\\left(\\theta \\right)}))&\\leq0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFor $r>0$, divide by $r$; the origin is included as well. Thus $0\\leq r\\leq - 3 \\sin{\\left(\\theta \\right)}$.\n\n**Angular bounds.** The upper radial bound must be nonnegative. The full disk permits $\\pi\\leq\\theta\\leq2 \\pi$. Choose $\\pi\\leq\\theta\\leq2 \\pi$. Every interior point is covered once.\n\n![Region, representative radial segment and angular sweep](images/af/af753b03aa012acb3b40fc8b1fe651b21033f2aebf214015f95dd3c6669457e3.png)\n\n**Transformed integrand.** Use $\\cos^2\\theta-\\sin^2\\theta=\\cos(2\\theta)$ in the difference of squares. The density substitution and area factor give\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x^{2} - y^{2}&=- r^{2} \\sin^{2}{\\left(\\theta \\right)} + r^{2} \\cos^{2}{\\left(\\theta \\right)}\\\\&=r^{2} \\left(- \\sin^{2}{\\left(\\theta \\right)} + \\cos^{2}{\\left(\\theta \\right)}\\right)\\\\x^{2} - y^{2}\\,\\mathrm{d}A&=r^{3} \\cos{\\left(2 \\theta \\right)}\\,\\mathrm{d}r\\,\\mathrm{d}\\theta\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore the required setup is\n\n$$\n\\displaystyle\\int_{\\pi}^{2 \\pi}\\int_{0}^{- 3 \\sin{\\left(\\theta \\right)}}r^{3} \\cos{\\left(2 \\theta \\right)}\\,\\mathrm{d}r\\,\\mathrm{d}\\theta.\n$$\n\n**Other choices.** $\\displaystyle\\int_{\\pi}^{2 \\pi}\\int_{0}^{- 3 \\sin{\\left(\\theta \\right)}}r \\cos{\\left(2 \\theta \\right)}\\,\\mathrm{d}r\\,\\mathrm{d}\\theta$: The area factor was divided out instead of multiplied in. $\\displaystyle\\int_{- \\frac{\\pi}{2}}^{\\frac{\\pi}{2}}\\int_{0}^{3 \\cos{\\left(\\theta \\right)}}r^{2} \\cos{\\left(2 \\theta \\right)}\\,\\mathrm{d}r\\,\\mathrm{d}\\theta$: These bounds describe a different disk or part-disk, with a different center direction. The area factor is missing. $\\displaystyle\\int_{\\pi}^{2 \\pi}\\int_{0}^{\\frac{3}{2}}r^{3} \\cos{\\left(2 \\theta \\right)}\\,\\mathrm{d}r\\,\\mathrm{d}\\theta$: The center-to-boundary radius is incorrectly used as a constant polar upper bound. $\\displaystyle\\int_{\\pi}^{2 \\pi}\\int_{0}^{\\frac{3}{2}}r \\cos{\\left(2 \\theta \\right)}\\,\\mathrm{d}r\\,\\mathrm{d}\\theta$: The center-to-boundary radius is incorrectly used as a constant polar upper bound. The area factor was divided out instead of multiplied in."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186707253 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0102cc08-e057-55e3-a286-f360b202f110" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\displaystyle\\int_{\\pi}^{2 \\pi}\\int_{0}^{- \\sin{\\left(\\theta \\right)}}r \\sin^{2}{\\left(\\theta \\right)}\\,\\mathrm{d}r\\,\\mathrm{d}\\theta" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186707227} {:answer/id #uuid "41e02736-b465-5ae2-89bd-091c8b798f11" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\displaystyle\\int_{\\frac{\\pi}{2}}^{\\frac{3 \\pi}{2}}\\int_{0}^{- \\cos{\\left(\\theta \\right)}}r^{3} \\sin^{2}{\\left(\\theta \\right)}\\,\\mathrm{d}r\\,\\mathrm{d}\\theta" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186707235} {:answer/id #uuid "7a6d6f05-025a-56af-9a63-b1bac2935f84" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\displaystyle\\int_{\\frac{\\pi}{2}}^{\\frac{3 \\pi}{2}}\\int_{0}^{- \\cos{\\left(\\theta \\right)}}r^{2} \\sin^{2}{\\left(\\theta \\right)}\\,\\mathrm{d}r\\,\\mathrm{d}\\theta" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186707242} {:answer/id #uuid "a99a2148-a21c-564a-b10f-62d4baa5a35c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\displaystyle\\int_{\\frac{\\pi}{2}}^{\\frac{3 \\pi}{2}}\\int_{0}^{\\frac{1}{2}}r \\sin^{2}{\\left(\\theta \\right)}\\,\\mathrm{d}r\\,\\mathrm{d}\\theta" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186707251} {:answer/id #uuid "e4efbbbe-df15-5ea7-8068-36c27345970c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\displaystyle\\int_{\\pi}^{2 \\pi}\\int_{0}^{- \\sin{\\left(\\theta \\right)}}r^{3} \\sin^{2}{\\left(\\theta \\right)}\\,\\mathrm{d}r\\,\\mathrm{d}\\theta" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186707256}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "41e02736-b465-5ae2-89bd-091c8b798f11" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\displaystyle\\int_{\\frac{\\pi}{2}}^{\\frac{3 \\pi}{2}}\\int_{0}^{- \\cos{\\left(\\theta \\right)}}r^{3} \\sin^{2}{\\left(\\theta \\right)}\\,\\mathrm{d}r\\,\\mathrm{d}\\theta" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186707235} :answer-field/id #uuid "011a7a5e-d126-5aeb-888a-a15fef263f31" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186707228}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "b4f0f3bc-82bd-563b-84cb-90c42857caf0" :question/problem "Let $D=\\{(x,y):(x+\\frac{1}{2})^{2}+y^{2}\\leq\\frac{1}{4}\\}$. Rewrite\n\n$$\n\\iint_D y^{2}\\,\\mathrm{d}A.\n$$\n\nSelect the polar-coordinate setup whose bounds cover $D$ once with $r\\geq0$ and whose integrand includes the area factor. Do not evaluate the integral." :question/worked-solution "Use $x=r\\cos\\theta$, $y=r\\sin\\theta$, and $\\mathrm{d}A=r\\,\\mathrm{d}r\\,\\mathrm{d}\\theta$. The full disk has radius $\\frac{1}{2}$ and center $(- \\frac{1}{2},0)$.\n\n**Radial bounds.** Expanding the disk inequality gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x^2+y^2+1x&\\leq0\\\\r^2+1r\\cos{\\left(\\theta \\right)}&\\leq0\\\\r(r-(- \\cos{\\left(\\theta \\right)}))&\\leq0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFor $r>0$, divide by $r$; the origin is included as well. Thus $0\\leq r\\leq - \\cos{\\left(\\theta \\right)}$.\n\n**Angular bounds.** The upper radial bound must be nonnegative. The full disk permits $\\frac{\\pi}{2}\\leq\\theta\\leq\\frac{3 \\pi}{2}$. Choose $\\frac{\\pi}{2}\\leq\\theta\\leq\\frac{3 \\pi}{2}$. Every interior point is covered once.\n\n![Region, representative radial segment and angular sweep](images/74/7422e92679515dcce0587cc6b518b259493ca13408d8be7c7f43e0dbcfd6e9a5.png)\n\n**Transformed integrand.** The density substitution and area factor give\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y^{2}&=r^{2} \\sin^{2}{\\left(\\theta \\right)}\\\\y^{2}\\,\\mathrm{d}A&=r^{3} \\sin^{2}{\\left(\\theta \\right)}\\,\\mathrm{d}r\\,\\mathrm{d}\\theta\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore the required setup is\n\n$$\n\\displaystyle\\int_{\\frac{\\pi}{2}}^{\\frac{3 \\pi}{2}}\\int_{0}^{- \\cos{\\left(\\theta \\right)}}r^{3} \\sin^{2}{\\left(\\theta \\right)}\\,\\mathrm{d}r\\,\\mathrm{d}\\theta.\n$$\n\n**Other choices.** $\\displaystyle\\int_{\\frac{\\pi}{2}}^{\\frac{3 \\pi}{2}}\\int_{0}^{- \\cos{\\left(\\theta \\right)}}r^{2} \\sin^{2}{\\left(\\theta \\right)}\\,\\mathrm{d}r\\,\\mathrm{d}\\theta$: The area factor is missing. $\\displaystyle\\int_{\\pi}^{2 \\pi}\\int_{0}^{- \\sin{\\left(\\theta \\right)}}r^{3} \\sin^{2}{\\left(\\theta \\right)}\\,\\mathrm{d}r\\,\\mathrm{d}\\theta$: These bounds describe a different disk or part-disk, with a different center direction. $\\displaystyle\\int_{\\pi}^{2 \\pi}\\int_{0}^{- \\sin{\\left(\\theta \\right)}}r \\sin^{2}{\\left(\\theta \\right)}\\,\\mathrm{d}r\\,\\mathrm{d}\\theta$: These bounds describe a different disk or part-disk, with a different center direction. The area factor was divided out instead of multiplied in. $\\displaystyle\\int_{\\frac{\\pi}{2}}^{\\frac{3 \\pi}{2}}\\int_{0}^{\\frac{1}{2}}r \\sin^{2}{\\left(\\theta \\right)}\\,\\mathrm{d}r\\,\\mathrm{d}\\theta$: The center-to-boundary radius is incorrectly used as a constant polar upper bound. The area factor was divided out instead of multiplied in."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186707260 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "40244d78-3088-5ffe-89a5-9b5338a17403" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\displaystyle\\int_{- \\frac{\\pi}{2}}^{\\frac{\\pi}{2}}\\int_{0}^{1}r^{3} \\sin{\\left(\\theta \\right)}\\,\\mathrm{d}r\\,\\mathrm{d}\\theta" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186707234} {:answer/id #uuid "4d33bad7-8aef-574f-9003-8907ad7595cf" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\displaystyle\\int_{- \\frac{\\pi}{2}}^{\\frac{\\pi}{2}}\\int_{0}^{2 \\cos{\\left(\\theta \\right)}}r \\sin{\\left(\\theta \\right)}\\,\\mathrm{d}r\\,\\mathrm{d}\\theta" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186707237} {:answer/id #uuid "8d2aced9-0763-50aa-9b66-19824e98f3a3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\displaystyle\\int_{0}^{\\pi}\\int_{0}^{2 \\sin{\\left(\\theta \\right)}}r^{2} \\sin{\\left(\\theta \\right)}\\,\\mathrm{d}r\\,\\mathrm{d}\\theta" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186707247} {:answer/id #uuid "e41382f4-0cfb-5856-a13c-5abaa89d012a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\displaystyle\\int_{- \\frac{\\pi}{2}}^{\\frac{\\pi}{2}}\\int_{0}^{2 \\cos{\\left(\\theta \\right)}}r^{2} \\sin{\\left(\\theta \\right)}\\,\\mathrm{d}r\\,\\mathrm{d}\\theta" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186707255} {:answer/id #uuid "f4e07afe-64af-5667-9a87-df2b24096b6b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\displaystyle\\int_{0}^{\\pi}\\int_{0}^{2 \\sin{\\left(\\theta \\right)}}r \\sin{\\left(\\theta \\right)}\\,\\mathrm{d}r\\,\\mathrm{d}\\theta" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186707259}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "e41382f4-0cfb-5856-a13c-5abaa89d012a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\displaystyle\\int_{- \\frac{\\pi}{2}}^{\\frac{\\pi}{2}}\\int_{0}^{2 \\cos{\\left(\\theta \\right)}}r^{2} \\sin{\\left(\\theta \\right)}\\,\\mathrm{d}r\\,\\mathrm{d}\\theta" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186707255} :answer-field/id #uuid "43dcb675-d3a1-5b3f-b5b7-aac7925fcc58" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186707236}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "fef636a2-c382-5f25-a554-fffa39593f5c" :question/problem "Let $D=\\{(x,y):(x-1)^{2}+y^{2}\\leq1\\}$. Rewrite\n\n$$\n\\iint_D y\\,\\mathrm{d}A.\n$$\n\nSelect the polar-coordinate setup whose bounds cover $D$ once with $r\\geq0$ and whose integrand includes the area factor. Do not evaluate the integral." :question/worked-solution "Use $x=r\\cos\\theta$, $y=r\\sin\\theta$, and $\\mathrm{d}A=r\\,\\mathrm{d}r\\,\\mathrm{d}\\theta$. The full disk has radius $1$ and center $(1,0)$.\n\n**Radial bounds.** Expanding the disk inequality gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x^2+y^2-2x&\\leq0\\\\r^2-2r\\cos{\\left(\\theta \\right)}&\\leq0\\\\r(r-(2 \\cos{\\left(\\theta \\right)}))&\\leq0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFor $r>0$, divide by $r$; the origin is included as well. Thus $0\\leq r\\leq 2 \\cos{\\left(\\theta \\right)}$.\n\n**Angular bounds.** The upper radial bound must be nonnegative. The full disk permits $- \\frac{\\pi}{2}\\leq\\theta\\leq\\frac{\\pi}{2}$. Choose $- \\frac{\\pi}{2}\\leq\\theta\\leq\\frac{\\pi}{2}$. Every interior point is covered once.\n\n![Region, representative radial segment and angular sweep](images/6e/6e255bbcda42c9a140bea788396762b33063295e40577ae82a62fc95ddcd763d.png)\n\n**Transformed integrand.** The density substitution and area factor give\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y&=r \\sin{\\left(\\theta \\right)}\\\\y\\,\\mathrm{d}A&=r^{2} \\sin{\\left(\\theta \\right)}\\,\\mathrm{d}r\\,\\mathrm{d}\\theta\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore the required setup is\n\n$$\n\\displaystyle\\int_{- \\frac{\\pi}{2}}^{\\frac{\\pi}{2}}\\int_{0}^{2 \\cos{\\left(\\theta \\right)}}r^{2} \\sin{\\left(\\theta \\right)}\\,\\mathrm{d}r\\,\\mathrm{d}\\theta.\n$$\n\n**Other choices.** $\\displaystyle\\int_{- \\frac{\\pi}{2}}^{\\frac{\\pi}{2}}\\int_{0}^{2 \\cos{\\left(\\theta \\right)}}r \\sin{\\left(\\theta \\right)}\\,\\mathrm{d}r\\,\\mathrm{d}\\theta$: The area factor is missing. $\\displaystyle\\int_{0}^{\\pi}\\int_{0}^{2 \\sin{\\left(\\theta \\right)}}r^{2} \\sin{\\left(\\theta \\right)}\\,\\mathrm{d}r\\,\\mathrm{d}\\theta$: These bounds describe a different disk or part-disk, with a different center direction. $\\displaystyle\\int_{0}^{\\pi}\\int_{0}^{2 \\sin{\\left(\\theta \\right)}}r \\sin{\\left(\\theta \\right)}\\,\\mathrm{d}r\\,\\mathrm{d}\\theta$: These bounds describe a different disk or part-disk, with a different center direction. The area factor is missing. $\\displaystyle\\int_{- \\frac{\\pi}{2}}^{\\frac{\\pi}{2}}\\int_{0}^{1}r^{3} \\sin{\\left(\\theta \\right)}\\,\\mathrm{d}r\\,\\mathrm{d}\\theta$: The center-to-boundary radius is incorrectly used as a constant polar upper bound. The area factor was squared instead of used once."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186707274 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "217b2c07-7135-5eb6-99c1-2ea1c3a9b2ce" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\int_{- \\frac{\\pi}{2}}^{\\frac{\\pi}{2}}\\int_{0}^{2}r^2(\\cos\\theta+\\sin\\theta)\\,\\mathrm{d}r\\,\\mathrm{d}\\theta" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186707267} {:answer/id #uuid "6291bac2-c032-554f-b3d8-043fb9d94803" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\int_{- \\frac{\\pi}{2}}^{\\frac{\\pi}{2}}\\int_{0}^{2}r^3(\\cos\\theta+\\sin\\theta)\\,\\mathrm{d}r\\,\\mathrm{d}\\theta" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186707275} {:answer/id #uuid "681a8ff6-8ae9-54bf-a622-77210912f808" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\int_{- \\frac{\\pi}{2}}^{\\frac{\\pi}{2}}\\int_{0}^{4}r^3(\\cos\\theta+\\sin\\theta)\\,\\mathrm{d}r\\,\\mathrm{d}\\theta" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186707278} {:answer/id #uuid "7e753db5-2166-5a17-985c-2f383509c29d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\int_{- \\frac{\\pi}{2}}^{\\frac{\\pi}{2}}\\int_{0}^{2}r(\\cos\\theta+\\sin\\theta)\\,\\mathrm{d}r\\,\\mathrm{d}\\theta" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186707283} {:answer/id #uuid "9ea030bd-8384-5aa7-a83f-6b5dac0f0724" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\int_{- \\frac{\\pi}{2}}^{\\frac{\\pi}{2}}\\int_{0}^{4}r^2(\\cos\\theta+\\sin\\theta)\\,\\mathrm{d}r\\,\\mathrm{d}\\theta" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186707287}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "9ea030bd-8384-5aa7-a83f-6b5dac0f0724" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\int_{- \\frac{\\pi}{2}}^{\\frac{\\pi}{2}}\\int_{0}^{4}r^2(\\cos\\theta+\\sin\\theta)\\,\\mathrm{d}r\\,\\mathrm{d}\\theta" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186707287} :answer-field/id #uuid "9a6a43c6-51d5-5ffc-9595-1e52a73adc75" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186707286}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "5e6d7448-7e1e-5cf5-86fc-822c1752ddab" :question/problem "![Shaded region centered at the origin](images/dc/dc01ad9cc9364f9cedd31909e482e58ffbd6f60d799e3d246d15134f510cb494.png)\n\nRewrite\n\n$$\n\\iint_D (x+y)\\,\\mathrm{d}A\n$$\n\nin polar coordinates for the shaded region $D$. Choose the setup whose bounds describe $D$ with $r\\ge0$ and whose density includes the polar area factor. Do not evaluate the integral." :question/worked-solution "Use $x=r\\cos\\theta$, $y=r\\sin\\theta$, and $\\mathrm{d}A=r\\,\\mathrm{d}r\\,\\mathrm{d}\\theta$.\n\nThe shaded region has radius $4$. Its polar bounds are\n\n$$\n0\\le r\\le 4,\\qquad - \\frac{\\pi}{2}\\le\\theta\\le \\frac{\\pi}{2}.\n$$\n\nThe transformed density is $g(r,\\theta)=f(r\\cos\\theta,r\\sin\\theta)r$.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}g(r,\\theta)&=((r\\cos\\theta)+(r\\sin\\theta))r \\\\ &=r^2(\\cos\\theta+\\sin\\theta)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe required polar integral is\n\n$$\n\\int_{- \\frac{\\pi}{2}}^{\\frac{\\pi}{2}}\\int_{0}^{4}r^2(\\cos\\theta+\\sin\\theta)\\,\\mathrm{d}r\\,\\mathrm{d}\\theta.\n$$"}]}
